Acropora acuminata	AACUM	AACUM.png	Acropora acuminata is a branching stony coral (family Acroporidae, order Scleractinia) distributed across the Indo-Pacific, particularly in the Coral Triangle and Great Barrier Reef. Its colonies form slender, tapering branches with small radial corallites, typically inhabiting shallow reef environments at 1–15 m depth. Like all Acropora, it depends on endosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae dinoflagellates for photosynthetically derived nutrition and calcification. This species is listed as Vulnerable on the IUCN Red List due to coral bleaching, ocean acidification, and reef degradation. Its genome has been sequenced as part of large-scale comparative coral genomics initiatives.
Acropora austera	AAUST	AAUST.png	Acropora austera is a colonial scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) found across the Indo-Pacific from the Red Sea to the central Pacific. Its colonies display corymbose to tabular growth forms with compact, tightly packed branches on exposed reef crests and upper slopes. The species maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae algae, which are critical for calcification and energy acquisition. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora awi	AAWI	AAWI.png	Its colonies form thin, tapering axial and radial corallites characteristic of the corymbose growth group in shallow reef habitats. The species relies on photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae and faces significant threats from bleaching and climate change. Scaffold-level genomic resources for A. It is listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN.
Acropora cervicornis	ACERV	ACERV.png	Acropora cervicornis, the staghorn coral, is a critically endangered branching coral (family Acroporidae) historically dominant in Caribbean reef systems. Its colonies consist of long, cylindrical branches forming extensive thickets in shallow waters (0–20 m), providing critical structural habitat for reef fish and invertebrates. Since the 1980s, populations have declined over 97% due to white-band disease, thermal bleaching, and hurricane damage. It is listed as Critically Endangered by the IUCN and Threatened under the U.S. Endangered Species Act. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora cytherea	ACYTH	ACYTH.png	Acropora cytherea is a dominant table-forming coral found throughout the Indo-Pacific, forming large, flat, horizontally spreading colonies exceeding one meter in diameter on upper reef slopes and outer crests at 3–25 m depth. Its impressive structure provides critical habitat for reef fish assemblages and contributes substantially to carbonate accretion. The species harbors Symbiodiniaceae endosymbionts and is highly vulnerable to elevated sea surface temperatures. Listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN, it has experienced marked declines across much of its range. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora digitifera	ADIGI	ADIGI.png	Acropora digitifera is a stony coral (family Acroporidae) widely distributed in the Indo-Pacific, forming compact digitate colonies with finger-like upright branches. Found in shallow reefs from the Red Sea to the western Pacific, it is commonly used as a model in thermal stress and bleaching experiments. It is classified as Vulnerable by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora echinata	AECHI	AECHI.png	Acropora echinata is a branching coral (family Acroporidae) distributed across Indo-Pacific reef systems from Australia to Japan, exhibiting corymbose growth with heavily spinose radial corallites giving branches a distinctively spiny texture. Like all acroporids, it depends on Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis and is sensitive to thermal anomalies. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora florida	AFLOR	AFLOR.png	Acropora florida is a colonial scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) occurring in shallow Indo-Pacific reefs from the eastern Indian Ocean to the western Pacific, growing in digitate to corymbose forms with elongated axial corallites in clear, well-lit waters. The species photosymbioses with Symbiodiniaceae and is vulnerable to thermal bleaching. It is categorized as Vulnerable by the IUCN.
Acropora gemmifera	AGEMM	AGEMM.png	Acropora gemmifera is a compact, arborescent coral (family Acroporidae) found on reef crests and upper reef slopes across the Indo-Pacific, forming dense clusters of short, upright branches with small, rounded radial corallites. Like other Acropora, it hosts intracellular Symbiodiniaceae and is highly susceptible to coral bleaching under thermal stress. The species was part of a landmark multi-species Acropora genome sequencing project contributing to phylogenomic analyses of coral diversification. It is currently listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN.
Acropora glauca	Acropora_glauca	Acropora_glauca.png	Acropora glauca is an Indo-Pacific scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) forming corymbose to digitate colonies with a characteristic pale or grayish coloration in live specimens. It occupies shallow reef habitats including reef flats and upper slopes, where Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis supports calcification and growth. Thermal bleaching and habitat loss pose significant conservation threats, and it is listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora hemprichii	AHEMP	AHEMP.png	Acropora hemprichii is a branching scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) primarily distributed in the Red Sea and western Indian Ocean, notable for its adaptation to the thermally variable, high-salinity conditions of that region. Its colonies form corymbose to tabulate structures in shallow reef environments (1–10 m) and have been studied for relatively higher thermal tolerance compared to Pacific congeners. It is assessed as Vulnerable by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora hyacinthus	AHYAC	AHYAC.png	Acropora hyacinthus is a dominant table coral across the Indo-Pacific, forming broad, flat plate-like colonies spanning several meters on shallow reef crests that provide critical three-dimensional reef structure and habitat. It is one of the most ecologically important Acropora species, contributing substantially to reef accretion and carbonate production. The species was included in a comprehensive multi-species Acropora genome sequencing project enabling comparative analyses of hybridization, symbiosis gene families, and adaptation. Notably susceptible to bleaching and crown-of-thorns starfish predation, it is listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN.
Acropora intermedia	AINTE	AINTE.png	Acropora intermedia is a branching coral (family Acroporidae) distributed widely across the Indo-Pacific, forming dense thickets of upright branches with intermediate-sized axial and radial corallites in diverse shallow reef habitats. Like all Acropora, it critically depends on its photosymbiotic relationship with Symbiodiniaceae for energy and calcification. The species faces conservation concerns related to bleaching, disease, and coastal degradation. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora loripes	ALORI	ALORI.png	Acropora loripes is a scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) distributed across the central and western Indo-Pacific, displaying a corymbose to tabular growth habit with characteristic lobed or rounded branch tips on upper reef slopes and crests. The species depends on Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis for the bulk of its energy requirements. Ongoing climate change represents a primary threat to this and other reef-building corals. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora microphthalma	AMICR	AMICR.png	Acropora microphthalma is a branching coral (family Acroporidae) distributed in Indo-Pacific reef environments, diagnostic for its minute radial corallites—a feature reflected in its specific epithet meaning 'small-eyed.' Its colonies inhabit shallow reef habitats and rely on Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis for nutrition. It was included in a multi-species Acropora genome sequencing study, providing comparative genomic data for understanding species boundaries and adaptive evolution within this diverse genus. Like all reef-building corals, it faces threats from ocean warming, acidification, and anthropogenic disturbance.
Acropora millepora	AMILL	AMILL.png	Acropora millepora is a model organism for coral biology, widely used in studies of bleaching physiology, gene expression, and host-symbiont interactions, with well-studied populations on the Great Barrier Reef. It was among the first Acropora to have its genome fully sequenced and annotated (2019), revealing extensive gene families associated with immunity, calcification, and thermal stress response. The species exhibits differential bleaching susceptibility among genotypes, making it central to identifying genetic determinants of thermal tolerance. It is classified as Vulnerable by the IUCN.
Acropora muricata	AMURI	AMURI.png	Acropora muricata (formerly A. It plays a foundational role in reef accretion and habitat provision for diverse reef fish and invertebrate communities. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora nasuta	ANASU	ANASU.png	Acropora nasuta is a corymbose Acropora coral with compact, rounded colonies distributed across shallow Indo-Pacific reef habitats, characterized by prominent axial corallites flanked by smaller, slightly upturned radial corallites. Like other acroporids, it relies on Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis and is vulnerable to thermal bleaching. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora palmata	APALM	APALM.png	Acropora palmata, the elkhorn coral, is a critically endangered reef-building coral endemic to the Caribbean, historically one of the most important shallow-water reef-builders in the region. Its antler-like, flattened branches can extend over 3 m, providing vital habitat for hundreds of associated marine species. Populations have collapsed by more than 90% since the 1980s due to white-band disease, bleaching, and hurricanes, triggering Critically Endangered status on the IUCN Red List. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora pulchra	APULC	APULC.png	Acropora pulchra is a branching coral (family Acroporidae) found across the Indo-Pacific, forming corymbose to bushy colonies in shallow reef environments with fine, tapering branches. Like all Acropora, it relies on Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis for carbon nutrition and is susceptible to bleaching under elevated temperatures. Complex interspecific relationships and morphological variability make this species valuable for integrative taxonomic study. It is considered Vulnerable by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora selago	ASELA	ASELA.png	Acropora selago is a corymbose scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) found across the Indo-Pacific in shallow reef habitats, forming compact, rounded plates with short, upright branches. The species maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae, critical for growth, calcification, and survival. Like most Acropora, it faces significant threats from ocean warming and reef degradation, and is listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora spathulata	ASPAT	ASPAT.png	Acropora spathulata is a branching coral (family Acroporidae) native to the Indo-Pacific, particularly the Great Barrier Reef, with colonies displaying a corymbose growth form and spatula-like, flattened branch tips—a morphological feature reflected in its specific epithet. The species inhabits exposed reef crests and upper slopes where wave action and high light intensity prevail. It is currently listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
