Actinernus sp. WN-2022	ASP1	ASP1.png	Actinernus sp. WN-2022 is an undescribed deep-sea sea anemone (class Anthozoa, order Actiniaria, family Actinernidae) collected from a deep-sea environment, representing a potentially new species typically found in deep oceanic benthic habitats attached to hard substrates or dwelling in soft sediments. Its notably large genome may reflect expansions of repetitive elements common in deep-sea invertebrates. This species provides valuable comparative data for understanding anemone evolution and deep-sea adaptation.
Actinia equina	AEQUI	AEQUI.png	Actinia equina, the beadlet anemone, is one of the most common and well-studied sea anemones in European coastal waters (family Actiniidae), a robust, solitary polyp found in intertidal and shallow subtidal zones capable of retracting its tentacles during aerial exposure at low tide. The species captures prey including small invertebrates and fish with nematocyst-laden tentacles and exhibits agonistic behavior including chemical and physical combat between neighboring individuals. Its genome has been sequenced, revealing insights into anemone immunity, venom gene evolution, and stress responses. It is also widely used in toxinological research due to its diverse suite of pore-forming toxins.
Actinia mediterranea	AMEDI	AMEDI.png	Actinia mediterranea is a sea anemone (family Actiniidae) closely related to A. Like its congener, it tolerates emersion during low tide and uses nematocyst-bearing tentacles to capture prey. The species shows morphological and genetic divergence from A. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Actinia tenebrosa	ATENE	ATENE.png	Actinia tenebrosa, the waratah anemone, is a brightly colored sea anemone endemic to the intertidal rocky shores of southern Australia and New Zealand, forming dense aggregations on wave-exposed platforms at the mid to high intertidal zone. It is ovo-viviparous, brooding its young internally and releasing juvenile anemones rather than broadcasting gametes. Its genome was sequenced and published in 2019, enabling studies of its potent actinoporin toxins and the genetic basis of thermal and osmotic stress tolerance. Actinia tenebrosa is widely used as a model for intertidal ecophysiology and venom research.
Actinodendron alcyonoideum	Actinodendron_alcyonoideum	Actinodendron_alcyonoideum.png	Actinodendron alcyonoideum, commonly known as the hell's fire anemone, is a highly venomous sea anemone (family Actinodendridae) distributed across Indo-Pacific reef environments. Unlike most anemones, it has a branched, tree-like body with multiple secondary branches bearing densely packed nematocyst-laden tentacle clusters capable of causing intense pain and skin irritation in humans. It typically inhabits sandy and rubble substrates near coral reefs at depths of a few to over 20 m. It possesses one of the most potent nematocyst-mediated stings among sea anemones. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Actinodendron arboreum	Actinodendron_arboreum	Actinodendron_arboreum.png	Actinodendron arboreum is a branching, highly venomous sea anemone (family Actinodendridae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef and sandy-bottom habitats. Its distinctive arborescent body plan features a central column with multiple branch-like projections bearing thousands of nematocyst clusters capable of inflicting severe stings. The species is nocturnal, typically expanding fully at night to feed on zooplankton and small prey. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Actinoscyphia liui	ALIUI	ALIUI.png	Actinoscyphia liui is a deep-sea sea anemone (family Actinoscyphiidae) described from deep-water environments in the western Pacific. Like other Actinoscyphia species, it is a large, solitary polyp with a broad oral disc and robust tentacles adapted to capturing prey including crustaceans and fish in low-food deep-sea environments. It is one of the few deep-sea anemone species with available genomic data, important for understanding molecular adaptations to extreme environments.
