Agaricia lamarcki	Agaricia_lamarcki	Agaricia_lamarcki.png	Agaricia lamarcki is a plating scleractinian coral (family Agariciidae) endemic to the Caribbean Sea, forming broad, horizontal plates in deeper reef zones and representing one of the most depth-tolerant Caribbean corals, dominant in mesophotic ecosystems from 20 to over 70 m depth. Unlike shallower congeners, it is adapted to low-light conditions and may maintain higher symbiont density to maximize photosynthetic efficiency. It is classified as Least Concern by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
