Alatina alata	AALAT	AALAT.png	Alatina alata is a cubozoan jellyfish (family Alatinidae) distributed across tropical and subtropical Indo-Pacific and Atlantic waters. Unlike most cnidarians, cubozoans possess complex, camera-type eyes with lenses, corneas, and retinas, enabling sophisticated visual behaviors including obstacle avoidance and phototaxis. Its nematocysts can cause severe stings including Irukandji syndrome in some regions, and a genome assembly has been produced for comparative studies of cubozoan vision and venom evolution.
Alvinactis idsseensis sp. Nov.	AIDSS	AIDSS.png	Alvinactis idsseensis sp. nov. is a newly described vent sea anemone in the family Actiniidae that flourishes in a variety of extreme deep-sea environments, particularly dominating the peripheral zones of sulfide-rich hydrothermal vents. Unlike shallow-water counterparts, this deep-sea species relies entirely on capturing chemosynthetic bacteria or specialized vent fauna—such as blind shrimp—for nutrition, rather than relying on photosymbiosis or chemoautotrophic carbon production from endosymbionts. While its precise phylogenetic position within the family Actiniidae remains a subject of ongoing molecular investigation, high-quality genomic data have been successfully generated for this species and are available in public databases. These genomic resources provide a crucial foundation for comparative studies on metazoan adaptation to toxic heavy metals, high hydrostatic pressure, and cnidarian evolution.
Anemonia sulcata	Anemonia_sulcata	Anemonia_sulcata.png	Anemonia sulcata is a large sea anemone (family Actiniidae) common in the Mediterranean Sea and eastern Atlantic, characterized by long, waving tentacles typically displaying green coloration due to endosymbiotic zooxanthellae (Symbiodiniaceae) providing photosynthetic nutrition in shallow, well-lit environments. The species produces a variety of bioactive proteins including toxins, GFP-like fluorescent proteins, and antimicrobial peptides, making it valuable for biotechnological applications. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Anemonia viridis	Anemonia_viridis	Anemonia_viridis.png	Anemonia viridis, the snakelocks anemone (family Actiniidae), is a photosymbiotic sea anemone abundantly distributed in rocky shallow waters of the northeastern Atlantic and Mediterranean Sea. Its long tentacles display a distinctive green or purple hue derived from endosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae and host-encoded fluorescent proteins that protect against photoinhibition. The species is a classical model for studying cnidarian-algal symbiosis in temperate settings and has contributed significantly to understanding the molecular diversity of GFP-like chromoproteins in cnidarians. Genomic resources and transcriptomic studies reveal extensive immune and symbiosis-related gene expression.
Anthopleura artemisia	Anthopleura_artemisia	Anthopleura_artemisia.png	Anthopleura artemisia is a sea anemone (family Actiniidae) found in intertidal and shallow subtidal rocky habitats of the northeast Pacific coast, able to host photosymbiotic zoochlorellae (green algae) or zooxanthellae depending on light availability, making it a facultative photosymbiont. The species forms solitary or clonal aggregations through asexual fission and is an important component of intertidal community structure. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Anthopleura elegantissima	Anthopleura_elegantissima	Anthopleura_elegantissima.png	Anthopleura elegantissima, the aggregating anemone, is one of the most abundant intertidal sea anemones on the Pacific coast of North America, forming dense carpets of clonal individuals on rocky shores from Alaska to Baja California. It is a facultative photosymbiont associating with either zoochlorellae or zooxanthellae depending on light levels, making it a tractable model for studying photosymbiosis flexibility. The species reproduces asexually through clonal division, forming genetically identical clumps that compete aggressively with neighboring genets. Its genome has been sequenced, revealing extensive immune gene diversity and gene families associated with symbiosis regulation.
