Anthopleura xanthogrammica	AXANT	AXANT.png	Anthopleura xanthogrammica, the giant green anemone, is one of the largest sea anemones on the Pacific coast of North America, with oral discs reaching up to 25 cm in diameter. It inhabits wave-exposed rocky intertidal and subtidal environments from Alaska to Panama, typically in crevices or tidepools with strong water circulation. Its brilliant green coloration results from endosymbiotic zoochlorellae and zooxanthellae that contribute photosynthate to the anemone's nutrition. This species plays a significant ecological role as a predator of mussels, sea urchins, and small fish in intertidal communities. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Antillogorgia americana	AAMER	AAMER.png	Antillogorgia americana is a sea fan coral (order Alcyonacea, family Gorgoniidae) found in shallow Caribbean reef environments, forming broad, flat, fan-shaped structures oriented perpendicular to prevailing currents to maximize prey capture efficiency. The species is a common component of Caribbean gorgonian communities and hosts photosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae in its tissues. Gorgonian corals are important structural components of tropical reef ecosystems and are increasingly studied for their pharmaceutical potential, including anti-inflammatory compounds. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Antipathes furcata	Antipathes_furcata	Antipathes_furcata.png	Antipathes furcata is a black coral (order Antipatharia, family Antipathidae) found in deep-water environments across the Caribbean and western Atlantic. Black corals are colonial anthozoans characterized by a dark, spine-bearing axis made of proteinaceous material, highly valued in jewelry and a focus of conservation concern. They typically inhabit depths from 10 m to several hundred meters, forming bushy colonies that serve as structural habitat for diverse invertebrates and fish. Antipatharian corals are among the longest-lived marine invertebrates, with some colonies estimated at thousands of years old. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
