Antipathozoanthus remengesaui	Antipathozoanthus_remengesaui	Antipathozoanthus_remengesaui.png	Antipathozoanthus remengesaui is a zoantharian anthozoan (family Antipathozoanthidae) that grows epizoically on black corals, similar to its congener A. The species illustrates the unusual and geographically widespread nature of the epizoic lifestyle on antipatharian hosts. Its morphology is characteristic of zoantharians, with cylindrical polyps lacking a skeleton and possessing two rows of tentacles around the oral disc. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Astrangia poculata	APOCU	APOCU.png	Astrangia poculata, the northern star coral (family Rhizangiidae), is a temperate scleractinian coral distributed along the eastern coast of North America from Cape Cod to the Gulf of Mexico. It is facultatively symbiotic with Symbiodiniaceae and can survive both with and without algal symbionts, making it a powerful model for studying the onset and breakdown of coral photosymbiosis. It forms small, encrusting or dome-shaped colonies on hard substrates in shallow to mid-depth waters. It also serves as a model for thermal physiology of temperate corals. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Astreopora myriophthalma	AMYRI	AMYRI.png	Astreopora myriophthalma is a massive to sub-massive scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae, tribe Astreoporinae) distributed across the Indo-Pacific, forming dense, rounded or encrusting colonies with small, circular corallites evenly distributed across the colony surface—distinct from most acroporids. The species inhabits reef slopes and lagoons at various depths and maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae. It is considered Least Concern by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Aulactinia verrucosa	Aulactinia_verrucosa	Aulactinia_verrucosa.png	Aulactinia verrucosa, the gem anemone (family Actiniidae), is a small, brightly colored sea anemone found in the northeastern Atlantic Ocean and Mediterranean Sea, inhabiting rocky subtidal environments in well-lit, current-swept areas. It is recognized by its verrucose (warty) column and colorful tentacles arranged in multiple whorls, and relies entirely on heterotrophic feeding without photosymbionts. It is occasionally maintained in marine aquaria and is a subject of research into cnidarian toxins and intertidal community ecology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Aurelia aurita	AAURI1	AAURI1.png	Aurelia aurita, the moon jellyfish (class Scyphozoa, order Semaeostomeae), is perhaps the most cosmopolitan and recognizable jellyfish in the world, with a translucent bell reaching up to 40 cm in diameter and four characteristic horseshoe-shaped gonads visible through the bell. The species has a complex life cycle alternating between a sessile polyp (strobila) stage and a free-swimming medusa, with blooms increasingly reported in eutrophied coastal waters globally. Genome resources have been published for multiple populations, contributing to understanding the molecular basis of its remarkable regenerative capacity and life cycle transitions. It is widely studied as a model for jellyfish bloom ecology and cnidarian developmental biology.
Aurelia aurita complex sp. Pacific	AAURI2	AAURI2.png	Aurelia aurita complex sp. Pacific represents a cryptic species within the Aurelia aurita species complex distributed in Pacific coastal waters, morphologically indistinguishable from Atlantic A. Like other Aurelia species, it exhibits a metagenic life cycle with both benthic polyp and pelagic medusa stages, frequently forming dense blooms in enclosed bays and estuaries with ecological and economic impacts on coastal ecosystems. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Aurelia coerulea	ACOER	ACOER.png	Aurelia coerulea is a scyphozoan jellyfish closely related to A. It forms large aggregations in semi-enclosed bays and estuaries, often associated with eutrophication and warming temperatures. The species undergoes the typical scyphozoan life cycle with polyp strobilation producing ephyrae that develop into medusae. Its blooms cause significant ecological and economic disruption in East Asian coastal fisheries and aquaculture. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Aurelia sp. 3 sensu Dawson et al. (2005)	Aurelia_sp_3_sensu	Aurelia_sp_3_sensu.png	Aurelia sp. 3 sensu Dawson et al. (2005) is a member of the cryptic Aurelia species complex, distinguished from morphologically similar moon jellyfish lineages through molecular phylogenetic analyses. It was one of several genetically distinct Aurelia lineages resolved in a landmark study that highlighted extensive cryptic diversity within this globally distributed scyphozoan genus. Its ecology and physiology largely parallel those of Aurelia aurita sensu stricto, with pelagic medusae developing from sessile polyps via strobilation. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Aurelia sp. 4 Dawson et al 2005	ASP3	ASP3.png	Aurelia sp. 4 Dawson et al 2005 is another cryptic lineage within the Aurelia species complex, identified by molecular markers as genetically distinct from A. Like other members of this complex, it is a typical scyphozoan with a transparent, disc-shaped medusa bearing four horseshoe-shaped gonads and marginal lappets, and may form seasonal blooms in coastal and estuarine habitats. Understanding the genomic distinctiveness of these cryptic species is important for accurate ecological and evolutionary interpretation of jellyfish bloom data.
Bergia puertoricense	Bergia_puertoricense	Bergia_puertoricense.png	Bergia puertoricense is a zoantharian anthozoan (family Sphenopidae, order Zoantharia) described from Puerto Rico and the Caribbean region, forming encrusting colonial polyp mats on hard substrates in tropical reef environments. The species belongs to the Brachycnemina, a major clade of zoantharians that includes species known for producing pharmacologically active secondary metabolites such as palytoxin. Its genomic resources contribute to understanding the evolution of zoanthid toxins and the systematics of Caribbean zoantharians.
Bergia sp. 'catenularis'	Bergia_sp_catenularis	Bergia_sp_catenularis.png	Bergia sp. 'catenularis' is an undescribed zoantharian (order Zoantharia) tentatively assigned to the genus Bergia, characterized by chain-like or catenulate colony growth in tropical reef environments. Zoantharians are soft-bodied colonial anthozoans closely related to corallimorpharians and scleractinian corals, forming major components of tropical reef communities. Members of this lineage may produce palytoxin or related compounds as potent chemical defenses. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Blastomussa wellsi	BWELL	BWELL.png	Blastomussa wellsi is a large-polyped scleractinian coral (family Lobophylliidae) distributed across the Indo-Pacific from the Red Sea to the central Pacific, forming low-growing phaceloid or plocoid structures with large, fleshy polyps in colorful shades of green, red, and brown. The species is popular in the marine aquarium trade and can host diverse Symbiodiniaceae genotypes. It is currently listed as Least Concern by the IUCN but faces threats from bleaching and collection pressure. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Bougainvillia cf. muscus	BCFM	BCFM.png	Bougainvillia cf. muscus is an operational taxonomic unit (OTU) representing a coastal hydrozoan (family Bougainvilliidae) closely resembling the highly polymorphic species Bougainvillia muscus. This marine invertebrate exhibits a dimorphic life cycle characterized by a sessile, branching colonial hydroid stage and a planktonic, sexually reproducing medusa stage. The hydroid colony forms irregular, tree-like or bushy tufts up to 120 mm in height, bearing fusiform hydranths with 10–18 tentacles surrounding a conical hypostome. The free-swimming medusa is distinguished by a cubic umbrella, branching oral tentacles, and four prominent marginal bulbs, each uniquely bearing approximately seven simple eyes (ocelli). Recently highlighted in evolutionary biology, the complete genome of Bougainvillia cf. muscus has been sequenced and assembled into a high-quality 375 Mb genome. This genomic dataset features thousands of predicted protein-coding genes and high macrosynteny with other hydrozoans, serving as a pivotal reference for investigating the molecular evolution of vision-related genes and early metazoan sensory systems.
Bougainvillia muscus	Bougainvillia_muscus	Bougainvillia_muscus.png	Bougainvillia muscus is a colonial hydrozoan (family Bougainvilliidae) distributed in temperate and boreal coastal waters of the North Atlantic and Pacific, forming branching, moss-like growths on rocks, shells, and algae in intertidal and shallow subtidal zones. The medusa stage is small, umbrella-shaped, and planktonic, with branching tentacles arranged in clusters at each corner of the bell margin. The species is representative of the diverse colonial hydrozoan fauna of temperate shorelines. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Briareum asbestinum	Briareum_asbestinum	Briareum_asbestinum.png	Briareum asbestinum is an encrusting octocoral (family Briareidae, order Alcyonacea) common in shallow Caribbean reef and rubble habitats, forming thick purple-brown encrusting sheets with densely packed retractile polyps that can overgrow adjacent corals and hard substrates. The species produces briarane-type diterpenes with potential anti-inflammatory and cytotoxic properties studied in marine natural products chemistry. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Bunodosoma granuliferum	Bunodosoma_granuliferum	Bunodosoma_granuliferum.png	Bunodosoma granuliferum is a sea anemone (family Actiniidae) found in shallow rocky habitats of the tropical and subtropical western Atlantic, particularly along the Brazilian coast and Caribbean, with a column covered in distinct adhesive verrucae that help it attach to substrate. The species has been used in toxinological and pharmacological research, with several bioactive peptide toxins isolated from its nematocysts. It is an important predator in rocky intertidal and shallow reef communities. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Bythotiara depressa	Bythotiara_depressa	Bythotiara_depressa.png	Bythotiara depressa is a deep-water hydrozoan medusa (family Bythotiaridae) found in mesopelagic and bathypelagic oceanic zones worldwide, typically transparent and gelatinous with long trailing tentacles for capturing prey in the water column. The species plays important roles in deep-sea carbon cycling and food webs as part of the gelatinous zooplankton community. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Calliactis parasitica	Calliactis_parasitica	Calliactis_parasitica.png	Calliactis parasitica is a large sea anemone (family Hormathiidae) renowned for its commensal relationship with hermit crabs, particularly Dardanus species in the Mediterranean Sea and Atlantic Ocean. The anemone voluntarily relocates to hermit crab shells, gaining mobility and access to food scraps while the crab benefits from the anemone's nematocyst-based chemical defense. The species displays complex behavioral repertoires during shell transfers, making it a model for cnidarian nervous system function. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Callogorgia gracilis	CGRAC1	CGRAC1.png	Callogorgia gracilis is a deep-water gorgonian coral (family Primnoidae, order Alcyonacea) found in cold, bathyal environments in the northeastern Atlantic and Mediterranean at depths typically exceeding 200 m, forming fan-like or tree-like colonies on hard substrates that provide important structural habitats for diverse deep-sea invertebrate and fish communities. Like other cold-water gorgonians, it grows extremely slowly and can be very long-lived. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Calvadosia cruxmelitensis	CCRUX	CCRUX.png	Calvadosia cruxmelitensis is a stalked jellyfish (class Staurozoa, family Calvadosiidae) found in temperate coastal waters of the northeastern Atlantic and Mediterranean Sea, attaching to seagrass blades, algae, or other substrates using an adhesive pedal disc. Its vase-shaped body bears eight tentacle clusters and it feeds on small crustaceans and invertebrates. Stauromedusae are unique cnidarians in that they are sessile rather than free-swimming, and a genome assembly was produced for C. Stauromedusae are valuable for reconstructing the ancestral body plan of Medusozoa.
Candelabrum cocksii	CCOCK	CCOCK.png	Candelabrum cocksii is a hydrozoan (family Candelabridae) found in cold temperate and boreal seas of the North Atlantic and Arctic, forming distinctive candelabra-like hydroid colonies with tentacle-bearing hydranths arranged symmetrically around a central stem. The species inhabits hard substrates and biogenic material on the seafloor, contributing to the diverse epibenthic fauna of cold-water ecosystems. Its life history includes both a sessile hydroid and a free-swimming medusa stage. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Carybdea cf. marsupialis auct. non (Linnaeus, 1758)	Carybdea_cf_marsupialis	Carybdea_cf_marsupialis.png	Carybdea cf. marsupialis auct. non (Linnaeus, 1758) exhibits a highly specialized morphological architecture tailored to its specific ecological niche. Macromorphologically, the adult specimen is characterized by a triannulate or segmented body plan, demonstrating bilateral symmetry with distinct anterior-posterior polarization. The outermost integument is composed of a chitinous or sclerotized cuticle, providing mechanical rigidity while simultaneously serving as a primary barrier against desiccation and environmental stressors. Anatomically, the locomotive apparatus consists of paired, jointed appendages articulating with the ventrolateral margins of the main trunk, which are heavily invested with sensory sensilla or tactile setae for environmental reception. At the microscopic level, histological examination reveals a pseudostratified epithelial layer resting upon a well-defined basement membrane, interspersed with glandular cells responsible for mucilage or biochemical secretion. Ultramorphologically, the cellular matrix is densely populated with elongated mitochondria and an extensive reticular network, reflecting an elevated metabolic capacity driving active physiological transduction.
Cassiopea andromeda	Cassiopea_andromeda	Cassiopea_andromeda.png	Cassiopea andromeda, the upside-down jellyfish (family Cassiopeidae), is distributed across shallow, warm coastal waters of the Indo-Pacific, resting bell-side down on sandy or muddy substrates and exposing its highly branched oral arms to sunlight to maximize photosynthesis by its endosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae. The species produces a mucus cloud containing stinging cassiosomes that can cause dermatological reactions in swimmers without direct contact. A genome has been assembled for C. Cassiopea species are increasingly studied as models for cnidarian sleep-like states and photobiology.
Cassiopea ornata	Cassiopea_ornata	Cassiopea_ornata.png	Cassiopea ornata is a benthic scyphozoan jellyfish (family Cassiopeidae) closely related to C. The species shows variable morphology and is one of several Cassiopea taxa subject to taxonomic revision using molecular data. Its pulsating movement facilitates water circulation and nutrient acquisition even in the sedentary, inverted posture. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cassiopea sp. PORT0000214	CSP2	CSP2.png	Cassiopea sp. PORT0000214 is an undescribed or provisionally designated member of the genus Cassiopea (family Cassiopeidae), representing a phylogenetically distinct lineage within this morphologically complex group of benthic, upside-down jellyfish. Like all Cassiopea, it harbors photosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae and inhabits shallow tropical coastal habitats including mangroves, seagrass beds, and reef lagoons. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cassiopea xamachana	CXAMA	CXAMA.png	Cassiopea xamachana is a scyphozoan jellyfish (family Cassiopeidae) distributed in shallow, warm-water lagoons and mangrove habitats of the Caribbean Sea and Gulf of Mexico. It is one of the best-studied Cassiopea species, providing a key model for investigating cnidarian photosymbiosis because its medusae can be experimentally cured of Symbiodiniaceae and then re-infected, enabling controlled studies of symbiosis onset, breakdown, and specificity. The species reproduces both sexually and asexually, with polyps undergoing strobilation to produce juvenile medusae. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Catablema vesicarium	Catablema_vesicarium	Catablema_vesicarium.png	Catablema vesicarium is a hydrozoan medusa (family Mitrocomidae) found in cold boreal and subarctic oceanic waters of the North Atlantic, a relatively large hydromedusa with a hemispherical bell and long marginal tentacles used for prey capture of copepods and other small crustaceans in the pelagic zone. Little is known about the benthic hydroid stage of this species, which is typical of many hydromedusae with poorly characterized polyp phases. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Catalaphyllia jardinei	CJARD	CJARD.png	Catalaphyllia jardinei, the elegance coral (family Euphylliidae), is a large-polyped scleractinian coral widely distributed across the Indo-Pacific, notable for its long, flowing tentacles with contrasting colorful tips that have made it highly popular in the marine reef aquarium trade. The species inhabits sandy and muddy substrates at the base of reef slopes and is listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN due to overcollection for the ornamental trade. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Catostylus mosaicus	CMOSA	CMOSA.png	Catostylus mosaicus, the blubber jellyfish, is the most common and largest scyphozoan jellyfish in Australian coastal waters (order Rhizostomeae), with a bell reaching 35 cm in diameter varying in color from blue to brown to white depending on Symbiodiniaceae content. Unlike many jellyfish, it lacks marginal tentacles and instead uses eight highly branched oral arms for capturing plankton, forming enormous blooms in estuaries and harbors along the east coast of Australia with significant impacts on aquaculture and coastal fisheries. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Caulastraea furcata	Caulastraea_furcata	Caulastraea_furcata.png	Caulastraea furcata is a colonial scleractinian coral (family Merulinidae) found in shallow to moderately deep Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming phaceloid (branching) colonies with elongated corallites and fleshy mantle tissue often extending beyond the skeletal rim. The species inhabits a range of reef habitats including lagoons, slopes, and turbid inshore reefs, maintaining photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cavernularia obesa	Cavernularia_obesa	Cavernularia_obesa.png	Cavernularia obesa is a sea pen (order Pennatulacea, family Cavernulariidae) found in soft-sediment habitats in shallow to moderately deep waters of the Indo-Pacific, with a primary axial polyp bearing lateral branches colonized by feeding and reproductive zooid polyps. It is bioluminescent, producing flashes of blue-green light when disturbed, possibly serving as a predator deterrent. Sea pens are important members of soft-sediment benthic communities and are particularly sensitive to bottom trawling disturbance. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Ceratomyxa sp. 57-BR2022	Ceratomyxa_sp_57_BR2022	Ceratomyxa_sp_57_BR2022.png	Ceratomyxa sp. 57-BR2022 is a myxozoan parasite (class Myxosporea, family Ceratomyxidae) assigned a provisional designation from a Brazilian specimen collection of marine fish parasites. Members of Ceratomyxa typically infect gallbladders and other internal organs of marine teleost fish, producing spores released into the water column to complete complex life cycles involving alternate invertebrate hosts. Despite their drastically reduced body plans, myxozoans retain key cnidarian synapomorphies at the molecular level, confirming their phylogenetic affiliation with Cnidaria. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Chironex yamaguchii	Chironex_yamaguchii	Chironex_yamaguchii.png	Chironex yamaguchii is a large cubozoan jellyfish (family Chirodropidae) distributed in coastal waters of the Indo-Pacific including Japan, the Philippines, and Southeast Asia, producing extremely potent venom capable of causing cardiac arrest in humans. Like its close relative C. Research on Chironex genomes contributes to understanding the evolution of lethality and vision in Cubozoa.
Chrysaora achlyos	Chrysaora_achlyos	Chrysaora_achlyos.png	Chrysaora achlyos, the black sea nettle (family Pelagiidae), is a large scyphozoan jellyfish with a bell exceeding 90 cm in diameter—one of the largest jellyfish in the world—periodically appearing in enormous aggregations off the coast of California and Baja California. The species feeds on zooplankton and small fish and is capable of causing moderate skin irritation in swimmers. Its episodic and poorly understood bloom dynamics make it an intriguing subject for marine ecology research. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Chrysaora chesapeakei	Chrysaora_chesapeakei	Chrysaora_chesapeakei.png	Chrysaora chesapeakei, the sea nettle (family Pelagiidae), is a scyphozoan jellyfish abundant in the Chesapeake Bay and mid-Atlantic coastal waters of the United States, forming summer-dominant blooms that impact water quality, clog fishing nets, and affect coastal recreation. The species alternates between a benthic polyp stage in winter and a pelagic medusa in summer, with strobilation producing ephyrae in spring. Its stings cause localized skin irritation and represent a public health concern in the Chesapeake Bay region. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Chrysaora fuscescens	Chrysaora_fuscescens	Chrysaora_fuscescens.png	Chrysaora fuscescens, the Pacific sea nettle (family Pelagiidae), is a large scyphozoan jellyfish with a golden-brown, striated bell reaching 50 cm in diameter, occurring in coastal and offshore waters of the northeastern Pacific from Canada to Mexico. The species forms seasonal blooms in upwelling zones where it feeds voraciously on zooplankton, fish eggs, and small fish, competing with commercial fish species for prey. Its blooms can be ecologically disruptive and interfere with aquaculture and fisheries operations on the Pacific coast. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Chrysaora quinquecirrha	CQUIN	CQUIN.png	Chrysaora quinquecirrha, the Atlantic sea nettle (family Pelagiidae), is a bloom-forming scyphozoan jellyfish found along the Atlantic coast of North America from New England to the Gulf of Mexico and Caribbean. It is a dominant summer jellyfish in the Chesapeake Bay, where dense blooms exert substantial predatory pressure on fish larvae and zooplankton and interfere with coastal recreation. The species has a benthic polyp stage that overwinters on hard substrates, producing medusae by strobilation in spring. A genome has been sequenced and deposited for C. Its sting causes moderate to severe skin irritation.
Chrysogorgia sp. JL179-B06	CSP1	CSP1.png	Chrysogorgia sp. JL179-B06 is an undescribed deep-sea gorgonian coral (family Chrysogorgiidae, order Alcyonacea) collected from bathyal environments in the western Pacific, belonging to a group characterized by distinctive golden to iridescent metallic coloration of the proteinaceous axis at depths of hundreds to thousands of meters. These slow-growing, long-lived corals form important structural habitats in deep-sea ecosystems supporting diverse associated fauna. Deep-sea gorgonians are threatened by bottom trawling and climate-related changes in deep ocean chemistry. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Churabana kuroshioae	Churabana_kuroshioae	Churabana_kuroshioae.png	Churabana kuroshioae is a recently described zoantharian anthozoan (order Zoantharia) from the tropical Indo-Pacific, named with reference to the Kuroshio Current region where it was collected. It forms encrusting colonial or semi-colonial polyp aggregations on reef substrates and is known for producing palytoxin and related zoanthamine alkaloids among the most potent marine natural products. The species contributes to understanding the biogeography and diversification of tropical Indo-Pacific zoantharian faunas. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cladocora caespitosa	Cladocora_caespitosa	Cladocora_caespitosa.png	Cladocora caespitosa, the cushion coral, is the only reef-building scleractinian coral endemic to the Mediterranean Sea, forming small bioherm structures at depths from a few to over 50 m. It is particularly notable as a survivor of past mass bleaching events in the Mediterranean, with populations displaying significant thermal tolerance variation that makes it a subject of active climate adaptation research. The species forms aggregations of phaceloid corallites with a compact, regular growth form on rocky and sedimentary substrates. It is assessed as Vulnerable by the IUCN and is a priority species for Mediterranean coral reef conservation. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cladonema pacificum	Cladonema_pacificum	Cladonema_pacificum.png	Cladonema pacificum is a colonial hydrozoan (family Cladonematidae) distributed in shallow coastal waters of the western Pacific, with hydroid stages forming small branching colonies and medusa stages distinctive for their branched tentacles bearing adhesive tips for prey capture, enabling a crawling behavior on surfaces unusual for free-swimming medusae. The species is a model for investigating hydrozoan regenerative capacity and the evolution of tentacle-based locomotion. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cladonema radiatum	Cladonema_radiatum	Cladonema_radiatum.png	Cladonema radiatum is a hydrozoan (family Cladonematidae) found in coastal waters of the northeastern Atlantic and Mediterranean, alternating between a branching hydroid colony stage and a free-swimming but crawling medusa stage with adhesive branched tentacle tips. The species has been used as a model system for studying hydrozoan regeneration, cnidarian biology, and the evolutionary origins of medusa complexity. Its accessible biology and small size make it a promising model for laboratory studies of hydrozoan development and regeneration. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cladopsammia gracilis	CGRAC2	CGRAC2.png	Cladopsammia gracilis is an azooxanthellate (non-photosymbiotic) scleractinian coral (family Dendrophylliidae) found in deeper, low-light reef and slope environments across the Indo-Pacific, obtaining nutrition entirely through heterotrophic feeding on plankton and particulate organic matter. Its colonies form small, phaceloid structures with elongated cylindrical corallites bearing large polyps that extend conspicuously in shaded habitats including caves and overhangs. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Clytia hemisphaerica	CHEMI	CHEMI.png	Clytia hemisphaerica is an established model organism for hydrozoan biology (family Campanulariidae) distributed in temperate coastal seas worldwide, with a well-characterized life cycle alternating between a colonial branching hydroid stage and free-swimming, transparent medusae that produce eggs and sperm for sexual reproduction. The species is highly amenable to laboratory cultivation and genetic manipulation, making it one of the key models for studying cell differentiation, regeneration, and gametogenesis in cnidarians. The Clytia genome was the first hydrozoan genome published with full annotation (2020, Institut de la Mer de Villefranche). Its photoreceptor biology, circadian clock, and stem cell biology have been extensively characterized.
Colpophyllia natans	CNATA	CNATA.png	Colpophyllia natans, the boulder brain coral (family Mussidae), is a massive scleractinian coral forming large, hemispherical colonies with meandering valleys and ridges characteristic of Caribbean reef crests and slopes, with some colonies estimated to live for centuries. It is a major contributor to reef framework construction in the Caribbean and is increasingly impacted by stony coral tissue loss disease (SCTLD). It is assessed as Least Concern by the IUCN but remains vulnerable to bleaching, disease, and storm damage. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Condylactis gigantea	CGIGA	CGIGA.png	Condylactis gigantea, the giant Caribbean anemone (family Actiniidae), is one of the largest and most conspicuous sea anemones in the tropical Atlantic, with thick, columnar body and long pastel-tipped tentacles spanning over 30 cm that provide shelter for commensal shrimp and fish species. The species harbors Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts and is important in the ornamental marine aquarium trade. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Corallium rubrum	Corallium_rubrum	Corallium_rubrum.png	Corallium rubrum, the red coral (family Coralliidae, order Alcyonacea), is a highly valued precious coral endemic to the Mediterranean Sea and adjacent Atlantic waters, with its characteristic blood-red calcareous axis prized for jewelry and decorative objects for millennia—making it one of the most commercially exploited marine invertebrates. The species forms branching colonies at depths of 10–300 m and is extremely slow-growing at under 1 mm per year skeletal growth rate. It is assessed as Endangered by the IUCN due to centuries of overharvesting. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Corynactis viridis	Corynactis_viridis	Corynactis_viridis.png	Corynactis viridis, the jewel anemone (order Corallimorpharia, family Corallimorphidae), is found in dense, colorful aggregations on rocky substrates in the northeastern Atlantic and Mediterranean Sea. Despite its anemone-like appearance, it lacks a calcified skeleton but is phylogenetically closer to scleractinians than to true anemones. Its striking colors ranging from red to orange, green, purple, and white result from host-encoded pigments and fluorescent proteins rather than photosymbionts. It reproduces primarily asexually through clonal splitting, forming extensive single-clone carpets on rocky walls. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Craspedacusta sowerbii	Craspedacusta_sowerbii	Craspedacusta_sowerbii.png	Craspedacusta sowerbii, the freshwater jellyfish (family Olindiidae), is notable as one of the only jellyfish species inhabiting fresh water, originally from the Yangtze River basin of China but spread globally through the aquarium trade and other vectors, becoming established in lakes and reservoirs across North America, Europe, and beyond. Its tiny medusae (up to 2.5 cm) appear episodically, often in large numbers. A genome assembly has been produced, providing resources for studying the invasion biology and freshwater adaptation of this unusual cnidarian. Its microscopic polyp stage is rarely observed, making medusa blooms the primary indicator of its presence.
Cryptodendrum adhaesivum	Cryptodendrum_adhaesivum	Cryptodendrum_adhaesivum.png	Cryptodendrum adhaesivum, the pizza anemone (family Thalassianthidae), is found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments with highly adhesive tentacles arranged in two distinct morphological zones covering the oral disc. The species is notable for its symbiotic relationship with clownfish, particularly Amphiprion clarkii, across the Indo-Pacific. Its distinctive two-zone tentacle organization distinguishes it from other host anemone species. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Cyphastrea salae	CSALA	CSALA.png	Cyphastrea salae is a massive to encrusting scleractinian coral (family Merulinidae) found across the Indo-Pacific in shallow reef environments, with small, densely packed corallites and characteristic septal morphology. The species maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae and inhabits a variety of reef microhabitats including slopes, flats, and lagoons. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Dendrogyra cylindrus	DCYLI	DCYLI.png	Dendrogyra cylindrus, the pillar coral (family Merulinidae), is a distinctive scleractinian coral endemic to the Caribbean Sea, forming tall, upright pillars that can reach 2–3 m in height and providing important structural habitat for reef fish and invertebrates. It is listed as Critically Endangered by the IUCN following dramatic population declines due to stony coral tissue loss disease (SCTLD), which spreads rapidly and with near-total lethality. A genome was assembled as part of a multi-species Caribbean coral genomics project, providing resources for disease resistance research. Active restoration efforts are underway in Florida to conserve remaining populations.
Dendronephthya gigantea	DGIGA	DGIGA.png	Dendronephthya gigantea is a massive, arborescent soft coral (family Nephtheidae) widely distributed across the temperate and subtropical rocky reefs of the Indo-Pacific, with dense assemblages notably occurring around Japan and the Korean Peninsula. Morphologically, it forms large, tree-like arborescent colonies that can reach up to one meter in height, supported entirely by hydrostatic pressure and a dense internal matrix of heavily calcified, spindle-shaped skeletal elements called sclerites. As an asymbiotic (azooxanthellate) octocoral, this species lacks photosynthetic symbionts and relies exclusively on obligate suspension feeding, deploying thousands of translucent, eight-tentacled polyps to capture phytoplankton from strong, nutrient-rich tidal currents. Characterized by striking, vivid coloration ranging from bright orange to deep crimson—produced by pigmented sclerites—D. gigantea serves as a crucial structural ecosystem engineer, creating complex, three-dimensional benthic habitats that harbor diverse assemblages of associated marine micro-invertebrates and fish larvae.
Dendrophyllia cribrosa	DCRIB	DCRIB.png	Dendrophyllia cribrosa is an azooxanthellate colonial scleractinian coral (family Dendrophylliidae) distributed in the Indo-Pacific, feeding heterotrophically on plankton and enabling it to thrive in shaded, deeper habitats without photosymbiosis. Its phaceloid colonies consist of elongated corallites bearing large orange or yellow polyps that expand conspicuously at night. It is an important component of deep-reef and cave ecosystems. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Desmophyllum pertusum	DPERT	DPERT.png	Desmophyllum pertusum (formerly Lophelia pertusa) is the dominant cold-water reef-building coral globally, forming vast three-dimensional biogenic reef structures at depths of 200–1000 m in cold, aphotic waters worldwide that support remarkable biodiversity with hundreds of associated species documented per reef. The species is long-lived and extremely slow-growing, with reefs taking centuries to millennia to develop and being highly vulnerable to bottom trawling, oil and gas development, and ocean acidification. Conservation of cold-water coral reefs is a major focus of deep-sea management globally. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Diadumene cincta	Diadumene_cincta	Diadumene_cincta.png	Diadumene cincta is a small sea anemone (family Diadumenidae) native to the North Pacific but widely introduced into harbors and bays worldwide, becoming an established invasive species in temperate Atlantic and European waters by rapidly colonizing artificial substrates such as pilings, boat hulls, and mussel beds through asexual laceration. Its small body size, rapid reproduction, and broad salinity and temperature tolerance make it one of the most successful marine invasive invertebrates globally. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Diadumene leucolena	Diadumene_leucolena	Diadumene_leucolena.png	Diadumene leucolena is a small, pale sea anemone (family Diadumenidae) found in rocky intertidal and subtidal habitats of the North American Atlantic coast, reproducing asexually by spontaneous transverse fission (pedal laceration) enabling rapid colonization of bare substrates. The species native versus introduced status varies by locality, and it contributes to research into diadumenid systematics and invasion ecology. Research on this species advances understanding of the molecular basis of asexual reproduction and range expansion in marine invertebrates. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Diadumene lineata	DLINE	DLINE.png	Diadumene lineata, the orange-striped anemone (family Diadumenidae), is native to Japan but introduced globally through shipping and aquaculture activities, becoming one of the most successful marine invasive invertebrates established in harbors and estuaries across temperate Europe, North America, and Australia. The species reproduces primarily through pedal laceration—leaving behind fragments of the pedal disc that regenerate into new individuals—enabling extremely rapid population expansion on bare substrates. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Dichotella gemmacea	Dichotella_gemmacea	Dichotella_gemmacea.png	Dichotella gemmacea is a gorgonian coral (order Alcyonacea) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming densely branching, dichotomously divided fan-like colonies as filter feeders that capture plankton and suspended particles with polyps. The species is found on exposed reef slopes and crests in the tropical Pacific and Indian Ocean, contributing to habitat complexity for associated fish and invertebrates. Indo-Pacific gorgonians including Dichotella are increasingly surveyed for secondary metabolites with pharmacological potential. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Diploria labyrinthiformis	Diploria_labyrinthiformis	Diploria_labyrinthiformis.png	Diploria labyrinthiformis, the grooved brain coral (family Mussidae), is a massive dome-forming scleractinian coral widespread on Caribbean reefs, characterized by sinuous valleys and ridges resembling brain folds with large polyps extending along shared valleys. The species is one of the most prominent reef-builders in the Caribbean and is notable for its long lifespan, with some colonies estimated to be over 500 years old. It is considered Least Concern by the IUCN, though it remains vulnerable to bleaching, disease, and storm damage. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Duncanopsammia axifuga	DAXIF	DAXIF.png	Duncanopsammia axifuga is a phaceloid scleractinian coral (family Dendrophylliidae) native to shallow turbid reef environments of the Indo-Pacific, particularly the inner Great Barrier Reef, with widely separated corallites on elongated stalks bearing large, fleshy polyps. Unusually for its family, it is zooxanthellate (harboring Symbiodiniaceae) and thrives in the silty, turbid inshore conditions where many other corals are less competitive. The species is popular in reef aquaria and is commercially propagated for the ornamental trade. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Echinopora horrida	EHORR	EHORR.png	Echinopora horrida is a plating and branching scleractinian coral (family Merulinidae) distributed across the tropical Indo-Pacific, with colonies forming thin, expansive plates or irregular branching structures with distinctive spiny projections and widely spaced corallites—features from which its epithet ('horrida', meaning rough or bristly) derives. The species maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae and inhabits diverse reef microhabitats. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Edwardsia elegans	EELEG	EELEG.png	Edwardsia elegans is a tube-dwelling sea anemone (family Edwardsiidae) found in soft sediments along the Atlantic coast of North America, lacking a pedal disc and instead creating a mucous tube anchored in soft sediment with only the tentacle-bearing oral disc extending into the water column for feeding. The species is studied in the context of cnidarian phylogenetics as a member of one of the most basal actiniarian families, providing insights into early sea anemone evolution and body plan origins. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Entacmaea quadricolor	Entacmaea_quadricolor	Entacmaea_quadricolor.png	Entacmaea quadricolor, the bubble-tip anemone (family Actiniidae), is one of the most widely kept sea anemones in the marine aquarium hobby, valued for its distinctive bubble-shaped tentacle tips and its role as host to at least 13 clownfish species (Amphiprioninae) across the Indo-Pacific. It is found in shallow reef environments occurring in both solitary and clonal aggregating forms depending on light and current conditions. The species harbors Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts and tolerates a wide range of lighting conditions. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Epizoanthus fatuus	Epizoanthus_fatuus	Epizoanthus_fatuus.png	Epizoanthus fatuus is a zoantharian anthozoan (family Epizoanthidae) found encrusting sponges and other invertebrates in tropical and subtropical marine environments, with colonial polyps that incorporate foreign material into their mesogleal tissue. The family Epizoanthidae is notable for producing palytoxin and related compounds that serve as potent deterrents to predation. Understanding the evolution of toxin gene families across zoantharian families is a major focus of zoanthid genomics.
Epizoanthus illoricatus	Epizoanthus_illoricatus	Epizoanthus_illoricatus.png	Epizoanthus illoricatus is a zoantharian (family Epizoanthidae) that grows as an epizoic colonial polyp on sponges, gorgonians, or other invertebrates in tropical and subtropical marine environments, characterized by small polyp size and the incorporation of foreign particles into the mesoglea. Genomic resources are helping to resolve complex species boundaries within this morphologically challenging genus.
Epizoanthus planus	Epizoanthus_planus	Epizoanthus_planus.png	Epizoanthus planus is a flat-forming epizoic zoantharian (family Epizoanthidae) with thin encrusting colonies of small polyps on marine invertebrate hosts in tropical seas, incorporating substrate particles into the coenenchyme. The systematics and species boundaries within Epizoanthus remain an active area of research requiring integrative molecular and morphological approaches. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Epizoanthus ramosus	Epizoanthus_ramosus	Epizoanthus_ramosus.png	Epizoanthus ramosus is a branching or ramose epizoic zoantharian (family Epizoanthidae) forming colonies on gorgonian axes or other hard substrates in tropical marine environments, with polyps arranged along branching stolons. Molecular data from this and related species are progressively clarifying the systematics of this complex group. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Epizoanthus rinbou	Epizoanthus_rinbou	Epizoanthus_rinbou.png	Epizoanthus rinbou is a zoantharian (family Epizoanthidae) described from Japanese waters, forming epizoic colonies on gorgonians, sponges, or hard substrates in subtropical and temperate Pacific waters. Japanese zoantharians including Epizoanthus species have been subjects of natural products chemistry due to their production of palytoxin-class compounds, which are among the most toxic non-protein compounds known. The species highlights the high diversity of zoantharian fauna in the western Pacific. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Epizoanthus scotinus	Epizoanthus_scotinus	Epizoanthus_scotinus.png	Epizoanthus scotinus is a zoantharian (family Epizoanthidae) found in temperate to subtropical marine environments, typically encrusting sponges or other colonial invertebrates and forming colonies of small polyps connected by stolons while incorporating foreign particles into the mesoglea. The species is part of the broad zoantharian radiation in temperate Pacific waters. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Epizoanthus stellaris	Epizoanthus_stellaris	Epizoanthus_stellaris.png	Epizoanthus stellaris is a star-patterned epizoic zoantharian (family Epizoanthidae) forming colonies on invertebrate hosts including sponges and gorgonians in tropical and subtropical seas. The molecular systematics of Epizoanthus and related genera are being progressively clarified through multi-locus and whole-genome phylogenetic analyses. The species contributes to understanding the diversity and evolution of epizoic cnidarians and their chemical ecology.
Erythropodium caribaeorum	Erythropodium_caribaeorum	Erythropodium_caribaeorum.png	Erythropodium caribaeorum is an encrusting soft coral (order Alcyonacea, family Anthothelidae) found throughout the Caribbean Sea and tropical western Atlantic, forming thin red to brown encrusting sheets with small retractile polyps over rocky substrates, dead coral, and other hard surfaces. The species is known to produce bioactive diterpenes and other secondary metabolites with documented allelopathic properties. It is an important competitor for reef space in degraded Caribbean reef environments and a model for studying soft coral chemical ecology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
