Eunicella verrucosa	EVERR	EVERR.png	Eunicella verrucosa, the pink sea fan (family Gorgoniidae), is found along rocky submarine slopes in the northeastern Atlantic from Norway to West Africa, forming broad fan-like structures up to 60 cm across at depths of 10–200 m and protected under UK and European wildlife legislation due to its ecological importance as a habitat-forming species in cold-water rocky reef ecosystems. Research on its population genetics and thermal tolerance is critical for managing this vulnerable cold-water gorgonian coral. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Euphysa ruthae	Euphysa_ruthae	Euphysa_ruthae.png	Euphysa ruthae is a hydrozoan (family Corymorphidae) found in coastal and oceanic waters, forming solitary or lightly colonial hydroid polyps on soft sediments or hard substrates, with free-swimming medusae produced by budding. The family Corymorphidae represents early-diverging athecate hydrozoans important for understanding the evolution of hydrozoan coloniality and medusa production. Its genomic resources contribute to resolving the deep phylogeny of Hydrozoa and the evolution of the polyp-medusa alternation.
Eutonina indicans	Eutonina_indicans	Eutonina_indicans.png	Eutonina indicans is a leptomedusa hydrozoan (family Eirenidae, order Leptothecata) found in shelf and oceanic waters of temperate and tropical seas, with small transparent disk-shaped medusae bearing evenly spaced marginal tentacles and statocysts, feeding as planktonic predators on small copepods and invertebrate larvae. Understanding the molecular biology of these abundant but poorly studied hydromedusae is important for characterizing open-ocean biodiversity. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
