Heteractis doreensis	Heteractis_doreensis	Heteractis_doreensis.png	Heteractis doreensis (formerly Macrodactyla doreensis) is a large, photosynthetic host sea anemone (family Heteractidae) widely distributed across the shallow, sandy substrate and coral reef flats of the tropical Indo-West Pacific. Morphologically, the species is characterized by an expansive oral disc, typically spanning 20 to 30 cm and occasionally exceeding 50 cm in mature specimens, which bears long, snaky, and slender tentacles reaching up to 10 cm in length. These tentacles are densely crowded along the periphery of the oral disc but become markedly sparse or entirely absent around the central mouth, where prominent white stripes radiate outward onto the disc base. Notably, the tentacles often exhibit a distinctive tightly coiled or corkscrew morphology when submerged. The distinctive column is embedded with prominent verrucae (wart-like spots) and terminates in a bright orange to red pedal disc used for anchoring deep within sandy pockets or rubble crevices. Ecologically, H. doreensis maintains an obligate photo-symbiosis with endosymbiotic zooxanthellae for autotrophic energy production and serves as a vital mutualistic refuge for numerous symbiotic invertebrates and anemonefishes, such as Amphiprion chrysopterus and Amphiprion clarkii. Recent mitogenomic sequencing has fully assembled its circular mitochondrial genome (20,720 bp), providing robust molecular datasets for resolving evolutionary linkages and taxonomy within the superfamily Actinioidea.
Heteractis magnifica	Heteractis_magnifica	Heteractis_magnifica.png	Heteractis magnifica, the magnificent sea anemone (family Stichodactylidae), is one of the largest and most visually striking sea anemones in the Indo-Pacific with a column reaching up to 1 m in diameter, occupying exposed reef crests where it is the most widely distributed host anemone for clownfish, known to associate with at least six Amphiprion species. It is critically important in reef aquaria and marine eco-tourism worldwide. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Heteranthus verruculatus	Heteranthus_verruculatus	Heteranthus_verruculatus.png	Heteranthus verruculatus is a small, highly camouflaged sand-dwelling sea anemone (family Heteranthidae) widely distributed throughout the intertidal and subtidal zones of the Indo-West Pacific and the Red Sea. Morphologically, this species is relatively small, with an oral disc diameter typically under 25 to 75 mm, varying in coloration from dark olive green and greenish-brown to deep sepia. The species displays a distinct dimorphic tentacle arrangement: the marginal tentacles are smooth, pointed, and elongated, while the discal tentacles covering the upper oral disc are modified into inconspicuous, papilliform (wart-like) or knob-like structures. Its column is densely embedded with adhesive verrucae (suckers), enabling the animal to attach its pedal disc to buried coral rubble, rocks, or seagrass roots, while accumulating sand grains for physical protection and crypsis. Economically and ecologically, H. verruculatus hosts endosymbiotic zooxanthellae for autotrophic energy supplement and exhibits a notable propensity for frequent asexual reproduction via longitudinal fission, which often results in irregular clonal clusters and asymmetrical body plans within localized benthic microhabitats.
Hydra oligactis	HOLIG	HOLIG.png	Hydra oligactis is a freshwater hydrozoan polyp (family Hydridae) found in cool, temperate lakes and streams across the northern hemisphere, notable for its seasonal senescence—undergoing rapid aging and death in autumn following sexual reproduction, a phenomenon absent in other Hydra species. Unlike many cnidarians, it lacks a medusa stage and reproduces asexually by budding during most of the year, regenerating from any body fragment. The species is an important model for cnidarian aging biology, contrasting with H. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Hydra viridissima	HVIRI	HVIRI.png	Hydra viridissima, the green hydra (family Hydridae), is a freshwater hydrozoan notable for its obligate endosymbiotic relationship with unicellular green algae (Chlorella-like), which give it its distinctive emerald green coloration and provide photosynthetically derived nutrition. Found in ponds, lakes, and slow-moving streams globally, it is a small solitary polyp with typically 5–6 tentacles bearing nematocysts for prey capture. Unlike other Hydra, its photosymbiosis with intracellular algae makes it a valuable model for studying freshwater cnidarian-algal symbiosis distinct from the Symbiodiniaceae-based systems of marine corals. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Hydra vulgaris	HVULG	HVULG.png	Hydra vulgaris is the classical model organism of cnidarian biology (family Hydridae), a small freshwater polyp famous for its extraordinary regenerative capacity and apparent biological immortality under laboratory conditions. Its genome was among the first cnidarian genomes sequenced (2010), revealing unexpectedly complex gene content including many genes shared with vertebrates but absent from other invertebrates, reshaping understanding of animal genome evolution. The species continuously renews its cells through stem cell proliferation at the body column, and isolated cells can reaggregate to form functional organisms. A telomere-to-telomere complete genome assembly was published in 2024, the first T2T assembly for any cnidarian. Hydra vulgaris remains central to research on regeneration, stem cell biology, aging, and cnidarian innate immunity.
Hydractinia echinata	HECHI	HECHI.png	Hydractinia echinata is a colonial hydrozoan (family Hydractiniidae) growing on hermit crab shells in temperate North Atlantic waters, an important model organism for studying cnidarian stem cell biology, coloniality, and allorecognition—the ability of colonial organisms to distinguish self from non-self when two colonies come into contact. The species has totipotent interstitial stem cells (i-cells) that can differentiate into any cell type including gametes, neurons, nematocytes, and gland cells. Research on Hydractinia stem cells has significant implications for understanding stem cell evolution and regenerative medicine. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Hydractinia symbiolongicarpus	HSYMB	HSYMB.png	Hydractinia symbiolongicarpus is a colonial hydrozoan (family Hydractiniidae) found on hermit crab shells along the Atlantic coast of North America, serving as a major model organism for studying cnidarian allorecognition, where two colonies either fuse (compatible) or reject (incompatible) each other based on polymorphic allorecognition loci. The species also provides a model for sexual reproduction, coloniality, and stem cell biology in Hydrozoa. Its genetics has contributed significantly to understanding the origins of immune self-non-self recognition systems in early-diverging animals. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Hydrozoanthus antumbrosus	Hydrozoanthus_antumbrosus	Hydrozoanthus_antumbrosus.png	Hydrozoanthus antumbrosus is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) that grows epizoically on hydroid colonies, making Hydrozoanthus one of the few cnidarians parasitizing or commensal with other cnidarians—a remarkable lifestyle offering unique insights into cnidarian-cnidarian interactions. The Hydrozoanthidae is a small, phylogenetically distinctive family within Zoantharia whose host specificity for hydrozoans offers unique insights into the evolution of epizoic lifestyles and cnidarian-cnidarian coevolution. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Hydrozoanthus gracilis	Hydrozoanthus_gracilis	Hydrozoanthus_gracilis.png	Hydrozoanthus gracilis is a slender zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) growing epizoically on hydroid colonies in tropical and subtropical marine environments, with smaller and more gracile polyps than typical zoantharians adapted to life on the delicate branches of hydroid hosts. The family Hydrozoanthidae is unique within Zoantharia for its host specificity toward Hydrozoa, offering comparative material for studying the evolution of cnidarian-cnidarian symbiosis and the ecological dynamics of epizoic associations. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Hydrozoanthus sils	Hydrozoanthus_sils	Hydrozoanthus_sils.png	Hydrozoanthus sils is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) found in tropical and subtropical Indo-Pacific waters, growing epizoically on hydroids with small polyps adapted to colonizing hydroid stems and branches. The species contributes to comparative studies of Hydrozoanthidae evolution and epizoic lifestyle, and genomic comparisons across Zoantharia are clarifying the sequence of character evolution in this ecologically diverse order.
Hydrozoanthus tunicans	Hydrozoanthus_tunicans	Hydrozoanthus_tunicans.png	Hydrozoanthus tunicans is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) that grows on hydroid colonies and possibly tunicates in tropical marine environments, with polyps characterized by the typical two-cycle tentacle arrangement of zoantharians and possible incorporation of foreign material into the coenenchyme. Hydrozoanthidae represents a specialized lineage within Zoantharia with a unique ecological niche among cnidarian epizoics, and its genome provides material for investigating host choice, secondary metabolite production, and molecular evolution.
Isaurus tuberculatus	Isaurus_tuberculatus	Isaurus_tuberculatus.png	Isaurus tuberculatus is a zoantharian (family Isauridae, order Zoantharia) known for its tubular, elongated polyps with distinctive warty projections giving it a coral-like appearance despite lacking a calcareous skeleton, forming encrusting or erect colonies in shallow tropical marine environments often in shaded, high-flow areas such as reef crevices and under overhangs. The family Isauridae occupies an interesting phylogenetic position within Zoantharia and is studied in the context of corallimorpharian-zoantharian evolutionary relationships. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Isopora palifera	Isopora_palifera	Isopora_palifera.png	Isopora palifera is a branching scleractinian coral closely related to Acropora (family Acroporidae) found on exposed reef crests in the Indo-Pacific, forming thick rod-like branches adapted to withstand high wave energy on outer reef flats, and differing from Acropora in having multiple mouths per corallite structure. Its phylogenetic position at the base of Acroporidae makes it important for understanding the evolution and diversification of one of the most ecologically significant coral families on Indo-Pacific reefs. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
