Leptoseris scabra	LSCAB	LSCAB.png	Leptoseris scabra is a plating scleractinian coral (family Agariciidae) found at mesophotic depths (30–150 m) across the Indo-Pacific, forming thin, flat plates or scroll-like colonies as a specialist of low-light mesophotic reef habitats that host specialized Symbiodiniaceae clades adapted to low-light conditions. Mesophotic Leptoseris species are of growing conservation interest as potential deep refugia from shallow coral bleaching events, and their genomics reveal adaptations to the photosynthetically challenging twilight zone.
Lophelia pertusa	LPERT	LPERT.png	Lophelia pertusa (now formally Desmophyllum pertusum) is the dominant cold-water reef-building coral globally, forming vast three-dimensional biogenic reef structures at depths of 200–1000 m in cold, aphotic waters worldwide that support remarkable biodiversity with hundreds of associated species documented per reef. The species is long-lived and extremely slow-growing, with reefs taking centuries to millennia to develop and being highly vulnerable to bottom trawling, oil and gas development, and ocean acidification. Conservation of cold-water coral reefs is a major focus of international deep-sea management policy. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Madracis auretenra	MAURE	MAURE.png	Madracis auretenra is a branching scleractinian coral (family Pocilloporidae) found in the Caribbean Sea, forming branching colonies with small, closely packed corallites occupying an important phylogenetic position within Pocilloporidae and studied in the context of Caribbean pocilloporid evolution and symbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae on shallow to mesophotic reef slopes. Its genomic resources contribute to understanding pocilloporid diversification and the biogeographic history of Caribbean reef coral faunas.
Madracis senaria	MSENA	MSENA.png	Madracis senaria is a scleractinian coral (family Pocilloporidae) found in the Caribbean Sea, forming compact, encrusting to mound-shaped colonies and notable for being one of the only Caribbean representatives of the family Pocilloporidae, studied in the context of biogeographic disjunctions between Pacific and Atlantic pocilloporid faunas. The genus Madracis is a relatively species-poor lineage with interesting morphological variability across environmental gradients that provides insights into coral adaptation. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Madrepora sp. 1 SH-2022	Madrepora_sp_1_SH_2022	Madrepora_sp_1_SH_2022.png	Madrepora sp. 1 SH-2022 is an undescribed scleractinian coral (family Oculinidae) assigned a provisional collection label from a Chinese survey, belonging to a genus of primarily deep and cold-water azooxanthellate colonial branching corals capable of forming dense framework structures in aphotic environments. The complex taxonomy of Madrepora and related genera benefits greatly from molecular data, and this specimen's genome advances comparative cold-water coral genomics.
Madrepora sp. SY280-shi	Madrepora_sp_SY280_shi	Madrepora_sp_SY280_shi.png	Madrepora sp. SY280-shi is an undescribed or provisionally designated scleractinian coral (family Oculinidae or Madreporidae) collected from deep-water environments in China, likely azooxanthellate and an important structural component of cold-water coral reef ecosystems. Genomic resources for Madrepora species contribute to understanding deep-sea coral evolution and the molecular basis of cold-water coral framework construction in the absence of photosymbiosis.
Mastigias albipunctata	Mastigias_albipunctata	Mastigias_albipunctata.png	Mastigias albipunctata, the spotted jellyfish of Palau's Jellyfish Lake (family Mastigiidae), is a scyphozoan rhizostome medusa famous for its isolated population in the marine lake Ongeim'l Tketau on Palau's Rock Islands. This landlocked population has lived isolated from open-ocean predators for thousands of years, evolving reduced nematocyst potency as predator defenses became unnecessary. The species harbors photosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae and undertakes daily horizontal migrations to optimize photosynthetic exposure. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Mastigias papua	MPAPU	MPAPU.png	Mastigias papua, the lagoon jellyfish (family Mastigiidae), is a rhizostome scyphozoan distributed across shallow tropical lagoons and semi-enclosed bays of the Indo-Pacific. Its bell is covered in white spots representing zooxanthellate tissue masses, and its highly branched oral arms harbor dense Symbiodiniaceae populations providing photosynthetically derived nutrition supplementing filter feeding. The species is best known from isolated marine lake populations in Palau and other Pacific islands studied as examples of convergent evolution and symbiosis biology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Meandrina meandrites	MMEAN	MMEAN.png	Meandrina meandrites, the maze coral (family Meandrinidae), is a massive scleractinian coral endemic to the Caribbean Sea forming brain-like hemispheres with sinuous valleys and ridges on reefs. Its large colonies are among the longest-lived corals in the Caribbean, potentially surviving hundreds of years. The species is listed as Threatened under the U.S. Endangered Species Act due to susceptibility to stony coral tissue loss disease (SCTLD). Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Metridium farcimen	Metridium_farcimen	Metridium_farcimen.png	Metridium farcimen, the giant plumose anemone, is a large sea anemone (family Metridiidae) found on rocky subtidal and pier habitats of the northeastern Pacific coast of North America, capable of growing to over 1 m in column height with hundreds of delicate, plumose tentacles arranged around the oral disc for filter feeding on phytoplankton and small particles. The species reproduces asexually by pedal laceration, forming clonal aggregations around parent individuals. Metridium farcimen is an important suspension feeder and is studied as a model for large-bodied, long-lived actiniarian sea anemone biology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Metridium senile	MSENI	MSENI.png	Metridium senile, the plumose anemone (family Metridiidae), is a common sea anemone in subtidal rocky habitats of the northern Atlantic and Pacific, with a cylindrical column and plumose, feathery oral disc adapting it for filter feeding on suspended particulate matter including phytoplankton and small detritus. The species reproduces by both asexual laceration and sexual reproduction, tolerating a wide range of temperatures and salinities. Metridium senile is a model for studying cnidarian aging, reproduction, and subtidal community ecology across both Atlantic and Pacific coasts. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Micromussa lordhowensis	MLORD	MLORD.png	Micromussa lordhowensis is a large-polyped scleractinian coral (family Lobophylliidae) found in Indo-Pacific reef environments, particularly known from Lord Howe Island and eastern Australian reefs, forming encrusting to low-mounding structures with large, fleshy, brightly colored polyps in red, green, orange, and purple morphs. The species is popular in the marine aquarium hobby and is recognized for its striking coloration. Its ecology, genetics, and susceptibility to bleaching have been studied in the context of high-latitude coral reef conservation at the southernmost Indo-Pacific reefs. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Millepora alcicornis	MALCI	MALCI.png	Millepora alcicornis, the branching fire coral (class Hydrozoa, order Anthoathecata, family Milleporidae), is found throughout the Caribbean and tropical Atlantic, forming branching, blade-like, or encrusting colonies with a characteristic calcareous skeleton superficially resembling stony corals. Despite the common name 'fire coral,' it is actually a hydrozoan that secretes a massive calcareous exoskeleton; its powerful dactylozooid polyps armed with nematocysts cause intense burning skin reactions in swimmers. Fire corals are important reef-builders in the Caribbean. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Millepora complanata	MCOMP	MCOMP.png	Millepora complanata, the blade fire coral (family Milleporidae), is distributed in the Caribbean Sea and Gulf of Mexico, forming flat, blade-like or encrusting calcareous colonies with fused irregular sheets. Fire corals are important reef-building organisms in the Caribbean and are susceptible to bleaching due to their Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Millepora dichotoma	MDICH	MDICH.png	Millepora dichotoma is a branching fire coral (family Milleporidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, particularly common in the Red Sea, Indian Ocean, and western Pacific, forming irregularly dichotomous branches with a pale, cream-colored calcareous skeleton housing numerous stinging polyps. It is one of the major reef-building organisms in the Red Sea, where it can form dominant monospecific stands on shallow reef crests. The large genome size in Millepora species is a notable feature distinguishing this hydrozoan lineage from most other cnidarians and is an active subject of genome evolution research.
Mitrocomella polydiademata	Mitrocomella_polydiademata	Mitrocomella_polydiademata.png	Mitrocomella polydiademata is a leptomedusa hydrozoan (family Mitrocomidae) found in cold temperate and boreal oceanic waters, bearing multiple statocyst-bearing structures arranged in clusters around the bell margin providing excellent equilibrium sensing in the pelagic zone. The species is a planktonic predator of small crustaceans and invertebrate larvae. Its phylogenetic position within Leptothecata makes it valuable for comparative studies of hydrozoan medusa evolution and sensory system development. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Montipora cactus	MCACT	MCACT.png	Montipora cactus is a plating to encrusting scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) distributed across shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming thin, irregularly spreading plates with characteristic verricose (warty) coenosteum surface texture. The genus Montipora is the second most species-rich scleractinian genus and a dominant component of Indo-Pacific reef flat communities. Genomic resources for Montipora species are essential for comparative studies of reef-building coral diversification given their ecological importance across the Indo-Pacific.
Montipora capitata	MCAPI	MCAPI.png	Montipora capitata is a colonial scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) dominant in Hawaiian reef ecosystems, forming encrusting to plating colonies on diverse reef substrates from shallow reef crests to depths of over 25 m. It is one of the most studied corals in the Hawaiian Islands and a key subject for research on bleaching dynamics, tissue loss disease, and ocean acidification effects on calcification. The species harbors diverse Symbiodiniaceae communities and shows differential bleaching susceptibility among genotypes. It is an important model for coral reef conservation in the Hawaiian archipelago. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Montipora capricornis	MCAPR	MCAPR.png	Montipora capricornis is a thin, plate-forming scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) found across the Indo-Pacific, forming characteristic whorl-like or capricornian plate structures on deeper or shaded reef habitats that maximize light capture through photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae. Like other Montipora species, it is an important reef contributor and faces threats from bleaching, sedimentation, and crown-of-thorns starfish predation. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Montipora efflorescens	MEFFL	MEFFL.png	Montipora efflorescens is an encrusting to foliose scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) distributed across Indo-Pacific shallow reef environments, forming irregular thin plates or encrusting sheets with a warty surface texture and small embedded corallites characteristic of the genus. The species harbors Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts and is found across diverse reef zones including lagoons and outer slopes. Understanding the genomic basis of diversification within the ecologically important genus Montipora is a major goal of reef coral genomics.
Montipora foliosa	MFOLI	MFOLI.png	Montipora foliosa is a foliose (leaf-like) scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) forming thin, upright, undulating plates across Indo-Pacific reef environments, often at moderate depths where wave energy is lower and light penetration drives Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis. Its distinctive leaf-like morphology with small, compact corallites distinguishes it from more encrusting Montipora congeners. The species is susceptible to bleaching and disease and contributes to Indo-Pacific reef biodiversity and carbonate production. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Montipora grisea	MGRIS	MGRIS.png	Montipora grisea is an encrusting to foliose scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) found across the Indo-Pacific, typically displaying a grayish or muted coloration compared to many reef corals and forming thin spreading plates on reef slopes and lagoons. Genomic resources for understudied Montipora species like M. Like other Montipora, it hosts Symbiodiniaceae and is vulnerable to bleaching and sedimentation stress.
Montipora palawanensis	Montipora_palawanensis	Montipora_palawanensis.png	Montipora palawanensis is a distinctive, slow-growing small polyp stony (SPS) coral (family Acroporidae) restricted to the shallow, wave-exposed upper reef slopes across the tropical Indo-West Pacific. Morphologically, the species forms robust submassive, encrusting, or laminar/plating colonies that are visually dominated by remarkably large, prominent verrucae uniformly covering the coenosteum surface. These dome-shaped verrucae systematically fuse together into continuous, irregular ridges, creating a deeply textured, maze-like or gyrate skeletal topography. Individual corallites are deeply immersed and hidden within the valleys between these highly elevated ridges, only aligning in neat, parallel rows along the very edge of thin laminar plates. Naturally found in shades of uniform brown or deep blue, M. palawanensis exhibits high cellular plasticity, yielding celebrated aquacultured strains like the "Kung Pao" and "Beach Bum," which display striking combinations of iridescent golden-orange coenosteum contrasted by bright neon-green or cyan polyps. Ecologically, this species relies heavily on its obligate phototrophic symbiosis with endosymbiotic dinoflagellates (zooxanthellae) and serves as an important, albeit uncommon, structural contributor to complex benthic reef ecosystems.
Montipora sp. Colony 1 RG-2022	Montipora_sp_Colony_1_RG_2022	Montipora_sp_Colony_1_RG_2022.png	Montipora sp. Colony 1 RG-2022 is an undescribed or provisionally designated Montipora coral (family Acroporidae) assigned a laboratory collection identifier from reef environments sampled in 2022. Like other Montipora, it forms encrusting or foliose colonies on Indo-Pacific reefs with small corallites and Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts. Provisional specimen designations are common in large-scale coral genomics projects where morphological identification is pending formal taxonomic revision.
Montipora sp. UDUK0000274	Montipora_sp_UDUK0000274	Montipora_sp_UDUK0000274.png	Montipora sp. UDUK0000274 is a distinct operational taxonomic unit (OTU) representing an unclassified hermatypic small polyp stony (SPS) coral within the ecologically dominant family Acroporidae. Derived from reef biodiversity exploration initiatives, this specimen serves as a critical model for cnidarian evolutionary genomics. Morphologically, as a member of the genus Montipora, it follows an encrusting, laminar (plating), or submassive growth architecture supported by a porous aragonite coenosteum. Its microscopic corallites are deeply immersed between specialized skeletal ornamentations, and its tissue maintains an obligate endosymbiosis with photosynthetic dinoflagellates (zooxanthellae) for autotrophic carbon assimilation. This specific strain, cataloged under UDUK0000274, was selected for high-throughput single-molecule sequencing by the Wellcome Sanger Institute, resulting in the generation of the highly contiguous, chromosome-level reference genome assembly jaMonSpea1.1 (and its alternate haplotype data). This genomic asset, actively utilized in comparative marine biology, provides fundamental insights into gene prediction, functional annotation, and the molecular pathways governing coral skeletal calcification and climate resilience.
Morbakka virulenta	MVIRU	MVIRU.png	Morbakka virulenta is a cubozoan box jellyfish (family Chiropsalmidae) found in coastal waters of the Indo-Pacific including Japan and Southeast Asia, possessing multiple tentacles per corner of the cube-shaped bell bearing thousands of nematocysts capable of delivering painful and potentially dangerous stings. Its venom contains pore-forming toxins that can cause cardiovascular and hemolytic effects, and stings in humans may require medical attention. The complex, camera-type eyes of cubozoans like Morbakka are a subject of active research into the evolution of sophisticated visual systems in non-vertebrates. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
