Nanipora kamurai	Nanipora_kamurai	Nanipora_kamurai.png	Nanipora kamurai is an octocoral (order Alcyonacea, family Naniporidae) described from the western Pacific, representing a small, encrusting colonial soft coral with a highly reduced skeletal apparatus compared to most alcyonaceans. It belongs to a recently described family whose systematic position within Octocorallia has been clarified through molecular phylogenetics. A genome assembly was produced for N. Its discovery highlights the continued undescribed diversity of shallow-water octocoral fauna in the Indo-Pacific.
Nanomia bijuga	Nanomia_bijuga	Nanomia_bijuga.png	Nanomia bijuga is a colonial siphonophore hydrozoan (family Agalmatidae, order Siphonophorae) found in mesopelagic oceanic waters worldwide, forming elongated colonies composed of highly differentiated zooids specialized for propulsion, feeding, defense, and reproduction. Siphonophores are remarkable for their extreme zooid specialization and colony-level integration, making them one of the most physiologically complex colonial organisms known. A genome assembly was produced for N. Siphonophores play important roles in deep-sea food webs and carbon cycling.
Nanomia septata	NSEPT	NSEPT.png	Nanomia septata is a colonial siphonophore hydrozoan (family Agalmatidae) closely related to N. Like other physonect siphonophores, its colonies consist of a pneumatophore (gas float) at the apex and a nectosome of swimming zooids (nectophores) that propel the colony through jet propulsion, followed by a siphosome of feeding and reproductive polyps. The extreme division of labor among zooids in siphonophores makes them important models for understanding the evolution of individuality. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Nausithoe racemosa	Nausithoe_racemosa	Nausithoe_racemosa.png	Nausithoe racemosa is a coronate scyphozoan jellyfish (order Coronatae, family Nausithoidae) found in oceanic waters, with a distinctive bell bearing a coronal groove that separates the central disc from the peripheral lappets—a feature diagnostic of coronate medusae. Coronatae is considered the most basal order of Scyphozoa and is important for reconstructing the early evolution of jellyfish. Its polyp stage is a colonial, tube-dwelling form found in deep and cold water habitats, and the alternation between this polyp and the medusan stage has attracted evolutionary developmental interest. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Nematostella vectensis	NVECT	NVECT.png	Nematostella vectensis, the starlet sea anemone (family Edwardsiidae), is the premier model organism for cnidarian developmental biology and comparative genomics, a small burrowing anemone native to brackish estuarine habitats along the Atlantic and Pacific coasts of North America. Its genome was sequenced in 2007, revealing a surprising degree of complexity and conservation with bilaterian gene content—including Hox genes and signaling pathways previously thought bilaterian-specific—revolutionizing understanding of animal genome evolution. The species is highly amenable to laboratory culture, spawning induction, and microinjection, enabling functional genomic studies.
Nemopilema nomurai	NNOMU	NNOMU.png	Nemopilema nomurai, Nomura's jellyfish (family Rhizostomatidae), is one of the largest jellyfish in the world with a bell diameter reaching up to 2 m and a weight of up to 200 kg, forming massive blooms in the seas of East Asia, particularly the Yellow Sea, East China Sea, and Sea of Japan. These blooms cause severe disruption to fisheries operations, clogging and tearing nets, and damaging aquaculture facilities. The species feeds on zooplankton and fish eggs and is harvested for human consumption in parts of East Asia despite its enormous size. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
