Orbicella annularis	Orbicella_annularis	Orbicella_annularis.png	Orbicella annularis, the boulder star coral (family Merulinidae), is a massive dome-forming scleractinian coral historically one of the most important framework-building corals on Caribbean reefs, with hemispherical colonies reaching over 2 m in diameter and some individuals estimated to exceed 500 years of age. The species is listed as Endangered by the IUCN due to massive population declines from bleaching, disease, and ocean acidification. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Orbicella faveolata	OFAVE	OFAVE.png	Orbicella faveolata is a massive, slow-growing hermaphroditic broadcast spawning coral (family Merulinidae) that acts as a primary reef framework builder throughout the Caribbean Sea and the Gulf of Mexico. Morphologically, it forms large, robust skeletal colonies that can attain multiple meters in diameter, exhibiting distinct growth plasticities: developing rounded domes or undulating columnar forms in calmer, deeper zones, while transitioning into flattened, shingle-like plating growth forms along wave-exposed reef edges to optimize photosynthetic light interception. Individual corallites are small (2–3 mm in diameter), evenly sized, and formed via extratentacular budding, featuring thin walls and irregularly dentated costae. Genomically, O. faveolata represents a vital cnidarian model; recent advancements utilizing PacBio long-read sequencing have successfully assembled a high-contiguity de-novo genome framework consisting of 51 contigs and containing 32,172 predicted protein-coding genes, vastly improving BUSCO completeness scores. This chromosomal asset, integrated with comparative proteomics mapping across seasonal thermal shifts, provides critical evidence regarding innate immune transcript networks, heat-tolerant algal symbiont partitioning, and functional calcification pathways under active conservation management.
Orbicella franksi	OFRAN	OFRAN.png	Orbicella franksi is a massive, slow-growing, and long-lived scleractinian coral (family Merulinidae) that acts as a vital primary framework builder across shallow and mesophotic reef systems throughout the Caribbean Sea, the Gulf of Mexico, and adjacent western Atlantic waters. Morphologically, it forms robust, irregular boulder-like or encrusting colonies that can exceed multiple meters in diameter. The colonial surface is characteristically bumpy and uneven, adorned with scattered mounds, ridges, or white patch-like growth anomalies that distinguish it from its congeneric relatives. Individual corallites are plocoid, circular to conical, measuring approximately 2.5 to 4.0 mm in diameter, and are irregularly distributed across the colony with strongly developed, heavily exsert and dentated septo-costae. Coloration of the living tissue is highly mottled, ranging from greenish-brown and mottled orange to yellowish-brown, often punctuated with pale or unpigmented growing tips. Ecologically, O. franksi maintains an obligate mutualistic symbiosis with photosynthetic dinoflagellates (Symbiodiniaceae) to fulfill its autotrophic energy requirements.
Pachyseris speciosa	PSPEC	PSPEC.png	Pachyseris speciosa is a foliose to encrusting scleractinian coral (family Agariciidae) distributed across the Indo-Pacific, forming thin, horizontally spreading plates or whorled structures with characteristic parallel ridges and no visible polyp mouths on the surface—an unusual feature among scleractinians reflecting its highly modified polyp arrangement. The species inhabits shaded reef slopes, lagoons, and mesophotic environments and maintains Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Paleozoanthus reticulatus	Paleozoanthus_reticulatus	Paleozoanthus_reticulatus.png	Paleozoanthus reticulatus is a zoantharian anthozoan (order Zoantharia) representing a distinctive lineage that incorporates foreign material such as sand grains and sponge spicules into its mesogleal tissue, forming encrusting or semi-erect colonies in tropical to subtropical marine environments on hard substrates. Its placement within the zoantharian phylogeny has been clarified through molecular analyses. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Palythoa caribaeorum	Palythoa_caribaeorum	Palythoa_caribaeorum.png	Palythoa caribaeorum is a colonial zoantharian (family Sphenopidae, order Zoantharia) forming thick, encrusting mats on shallow Caribbean reef substrates, recognized as one of the most abundant and competitive space-occupying organisms on degraded Caribbean reefs. Its colonies incorporate sand and foreign particles into the coenenchyme, giving them a gritty, sandpaper-like texture. The species produces palytoxin and related compounds, among the most toxic marine natural products known. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Palythoa grandiflora	Palythoa_grandiflora	Palythoa_grandiflora.png	Palythoa grandiflora is a zoantharian (family Sphenopidae) found in shallow tropical Pacific reef environments, forming colonial encrusting mats with large, distinctive polyps that incorporate foreign particles into the coenenchyme. Like other Palythoa species, it is a potential producer of palytoxin and related bioactive compounds that are of significant toxicological and pharmacological interest. The species contributes to the structural complexity and competitive dynamics of shallow tropical reef communities. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Palythoa grandis	Palythoa_grandis	Palythoa_grandis.png	Palythoa grandis is a large-polyped zoantharian (family Sphenopidae) distributed in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming encrusting colonial mats with notably large, fleshy polyps that extend conspicuously from the sandy coenenchyme for suspension feeding. Like other Palythoa species, it can produce palytoxin, a highly potent marine toxin with significant medical and pharmacological implications. The species is frequently encountered in reef surveys across the Indo-Pacific and contributes to the benthic community structure of tropical reef flats. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Palythoa heliodiscus	Palythoa_heliodiscus	Palythoa_heliodiscus.png	Palythoa heliodiscus is a zoantharian (family Sphenopidae) found in shallow tropical reef environments, forming encrusting colonial mats with polyps incorporating foreign particles into the coenenchyme. The species belongs to the palytoxin-producing clade of Palythoa and is of interest for marine natural products research. Its genomic data contribute to understanding palytoxin biosynthesis, zoantharian systematics, and the competitive ecology of encrusting reef organisms.
Palythoa mizigama	PMIZI	PMIZI.png	Palythoa mizigama is a zoantharian (family Sphenopidae) described from the Indo-Pacific, forming encrusting mats of colonial polyps on shallow reef substrates and incorporating sand and skeletal material into the coenenchyme. Like other members of the genus, it may produce palytoxin and related compounds. The species is part of the diverse zoantharian fauna of tropical Indo-Pacific coral reefs. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Palythoa mutuki	Palythoa_mutuki	Palythoa_mutuki.png	Palythoa mutuki is a zoantharian (family Sphenopidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming encrusting colonial mats with a characteristic texture arising from the incorporation of foreign particles. It is documented as a producer of palytoxin-related compounds, making it of toxicological significance and relevant to human health risks associated with coral reef handling. Its genomics contribute to a comprehensive understanding of toxin diversity in Sphenopidae.
Palythoa umbrosa	PUMBR	PUMBR.png	Palythoa umbrosa is a shade-tolerant zoantharian (family Sphenopidae) found in shallow to moderately deep reef environments across the Indo-Pacific, typically inhabiting overhangs, crevices, and shaded reef walls where light intensity is reduced. Like other Palythoa species, it incorporates foreign particles into the coenenchyme and may produce palytoxin-related compounds. Its preference for shaded habitats distinguishes it ecologically from many congeners that occupy exposed reef flats. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Paracondylactis sinensis	PSINE1	PSINE1.png	Paracondylactis sinensis is a sea anemone (family Hormathiidae) described from Chinese coastal waters, inhabiting subtidal rocky substrates in temperate to subtropical environments of the western Pacific. Like other hormathiid anemones, it may associate with hermit crabs or other crustaceans in commensal relationships. Its systematic placement within Hormathiidae and comparative genomic data help clarify the evolution of commensal lifestyles in this actiniarian family.
Paragorgia papillata	PPAPI	PPAPI.png	Paragorgia papillata is a large, colonial deep-sea gorgonian coral (family Paragorgiidae, order Alcyonacea) found in cold, deep-water environments of the Pacific Ocean at depths of several hundred to over 1000 m. Known as bubblegum corals due to the distinctive spherical swellings on their branches, Paragorgia species form large, tree-like colonies that serve as important structural habitats and biodiversity hotspots in deep-sea ecosystems. These extremely slow-growing, long-lived corals are highly vulnerable to bottom trawling disturbance. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Paramuricea biscaya	Paramuricea_biscaya	Paramuricea_biscaya.png	Paramuricea biscaya is a deep-water gorgonian coral (family Plexauridae) found in the Gulf of Mexico, one of the species severely impacted by the 2010 Deepwater Horizon oil spill, with populations showing tissue damage, mucus production, and colony mortality at sites near the wellhead. This event generated significant research interest in the biology and resilience of cold-water gorgonian corals and their sensitivity to hydrocarbon contamination. It inhabits depths of 1000–2200 m on hard substrates in the Gulf of Mexico. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Paramuricea clavata	PCLAV	PCLAV.png	Paramuricea clavata is a Mediterranean gorgonian coral (family Plexauridae) forming large, fan-shaped or dichotomously branching colonies on rocky substrates from 15 to over 100 m depth across the northwestern Mediterranean Sea. It is one of the key habitat-forming species of Mediterranean coralligenous communities and has suffered catastrophic mass mortality events during anomalously warm summers, particularly in 1999 and 2003. It is listed as Endangered by the IUCN. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Paranemonia cinerea	Paranemonia_cinerea	Paranemonia_cinerea.png	Paranemonia cinerea is a small photosymbiotic sea anemone (family Verrillactiniidae) found in seagrass beds and shallow rocky habitats of the Mediterranean Sea and eastern Atlantic, living partially buried in soft sediments or attached to seagrass rhizomes. Uniquely among Mediterranean anemones, it harbors Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts providing significant nutritional input in well-lit shallow habitats. It is studied as a model for the diversity of photosymbiotic associations in temperate sea anemones. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Paraphelliactis xishaensis sp. nov.	PXISH	PXISH.png	Deep-sea anemone species Paraphelliactis xishaensis sp. nov. is a newly described species of deep-sea anemone in the genus Paraphelliactis, discovered in the Xisha Trough of the South China Sea at a depth of 3,230 meters. This species is adapted to the extreme conditions of the deep sea, thriving in cold, dark, and high-pressure environments. Its discovery contributes to our understanding of the biodiversity and ecological dynamics of deep-sea ecosystems.
Parazoanthus atlanticus	Parazoanthus_atlanticus	Parazoanthus_atlanticus.png	Parazoanthus atlanticus is a zoantharian (family Parazoanthidae, order Zoantharia) forming colonial encrusting polyps on sponges, gorgonians, or rocky substrates in the northeastern Atlantic Ocean and Mediterranean Sea. Unlike Palythoa and related genera, Parazoanthus species do not incorporate foreign particles into the coenenchyme and are not known to produce palytoxin, instead producing a different class of alkaloids (parazoanthines). Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Parazoanthus darwini	Parazoanthus_darwini	Parazoanthus_darwini.png	Parazoanthus darwini is a zoantharian (family Parazoanthidae) found in the eastern Atlantic and surrounding islands, named in honor of Charles Darwin and forming colonial encrusting polyps on sponges and hard substrates. Like other Parazoanthus species, it produces parazoanthine alkaloids rather than palytoxin-class compounds, reflecting the distinct secondary metabolite chemistry of this zoantharian family. The species contributes to understanding the biogeography of zoantharians in the eastern Atlantic. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Parazoanthus swiftii	Parazoanthus_swiftii	Parazoanthus_swiftii.png	Parazoanthus swiftii is a zoantharian (family Parazoanthidae) found in shallow Caribbean and Gulf of Mexico reef environments, forming epizoic colonies on gorgonian corals and sponges. The species was one of the earliest zoantharians studied for its secondary metabolite chemistry, and several bioactive compounds with cytotoxic and anti-inflammatory properties have been isolated from it. It is a common epizoic organism on Caribbean gorgonians and represents an important component of reef epibenthic communities. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Pavona decussata	Pavona_decussata	Pavona_decussata.png	Pavona decussata is a foliose to plating scleractinian coral (family Agariciidae) distributed across the Indo-Pacific, forming thin, upright, irregular plates with characteristic decussate (cross-hatched) septal arrangement visible on the colony surface. The species inhabits lagoons, sheltered reef slopes, and turbid inshore reef environments where it competes for space with encrusting algae and other corals. Pavona species are important contributors to mesophotic and turbid reef ecosystems often overlooked in reef biodiversity assessments. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Pelagia noctiluca	PNOCT	PNOCT.png	Pelagia noctiluca, the mauve stinger (family Pelagiidae), is a cosmopolitan scyphozoan jellyfish distributed throughout tropical and temperate oceans worldwide, known for its striking bioluminescence and potent sting causing severe dermatitis in swimmers. Unlike most scyphozoans, it lacks a benthic polyp stage entirely, completing its life cycle as a holoplanktonic organism with direct development from planula to juvenile medusa. The species forms extensive blooms in the Mediterranean Sea and Atlantic Ocean that cause significant economic damage to coastal tourism and aquaculture. A genome assembly has been produced for P.
Phacellophora camtschatica	Phacellophora_camtschatica	Phacellophora_camtschatica.png	Phacellophora camtschatica, the fried egg jellyfish (family Phacellophoridae), is a large scyphozoan medusa with a distinctive pale yellow and white coloration resembling a fried egg, distributed in cool temperate and subarctic waters of the Pacific and Atlantic Oceans. Its bell can reach over 60 cm in diameter, and it trails long, fine tentacles up to several meters. Despite its impressive size, it has a weak sting and preys primarily on other jellyfish including Aurelia species, making it an unusual jellyfish predator. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Phenganax marumi	Phenganax_marumi	Phenganax_marumi.png	Phenganax marumi is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) found in tropical Indo-Pacific marine environments, growing epizoically on hydroid colonies or other substrates. Like other members of this family, its polyps are small and adapted to colonizing the branches of hydroid hosts in a commensal or parasitic relationship unique among cnidarians. Its inclusion in comparative genomic datasets helps clarify the molecular basis of host specificity in epizoic zoantharians.
Phenganax stokvisi	Phenganax_stokvisi	Phenganax_stokvisi.png	Phenganax stokvisi is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) found in tropical marine environments, growing epizoically on hydroid colonies with small, gracile polyps adapted to this unusual lifestyle. The systematic position of Phenganax within Zoantharia has been clarified through molecular phylogenetics, revealing its relationship to other epizoic zoantharian families. Its genomic data contribute to understanding the evolution of cnidarian-cnidarian epizoic associations in tropical marine environments.
Phenganax subtilis	Phenganax_subtilis	Phenganax_subtilis.png	Phenganax subtilis is a slender zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) found in tropical Indo-Pacific waters, forming epizoic colonies on hydroid branches. Like its congeners, it possesses the distinctive two-cycle tentacle arrangement of zoantharians adapted to the compact morphology required for life on hydroid hosts. The three described Phenganax species collectively provide comparative material for understanding the genomic basis of epizoic lifestyle evolution within Hydrozoanthidae, a unique family of cnidarian-cnidarian epizoics.
Phyllorhiza punctata	Phyllorhiza_punctata	Phyllorhiza_punctata.png	Phyllorhiza punctata, the Australian spotted jellyfish (family Mastigiidae), is a rhizostome scyphozoan native to the Indo-Pacific but introduced into the Gulf of Mexico and Caribbean, where it forms large blooms that cause significant ecological disruption by consuming large quantities of zooplankton and fish eggs. Its bell is covered in white spots and harbors dense populations of Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts that supplement its nutritional intake from filter feeding. It is considered one of the most ecologically damaging marine invasive jellyfish species globally. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Phymanthus crucifer	Phymanthus_crucifer	Phymanthus_crucifer.png	Phymanthus crucifer is a sea anemone (family Phymanthidae, order Actiniaria) found in shallow Caribbean reef and rocky habitats, characterized by distinctive tentacles with branched, cross-like (cruciform) tips that give it its specific epithet. It typically inhabits crevices, rubble, and reef rock surfaces in shallow tropical Atlantic environments. The family Phymanthidae occupies an interesting phylogenetic position within Actiniaria and contributes to the diversity of Caribbean sea anemone fauna. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Phymanthus loligo	Phymanthus_loligo	Phymanthus_loligo.png	Phymanthus loligo is a sea anemone (family Phymanthidae) found in shallow tropical reef and rocky habitats of the Indo-Pacific, with characteristic branched tentacle tips similar to its Atlantic congener P. It inhabits sandy and rubble substrates in reef environments and may host commensal shrimps and other small invertebrates. Its genomic data enable comparative studies of tentacle morphology evolution and the biogeography of this distinctive actiniarian family.
Physalia physalis	Physalia_physalis	Physalia_physalis.png	Physalia physalis, the Portuguese man o' war (family Physaliidae, order Siphonophorae), is one of the most recognizable and feared marine organisms in the world, distributed in tropical and subtropical oceanic waters globally and frequently washed ashore on Atlantic and Pacific beaches. Despite superficial resemblance to a jellyfish, it is a colonial siphonophore composed of specialized zooids functioning as a gas-filled pneumatophore float, dactylozooids bearing nematocyst-laden tentacles that can extend up to 50 m, and reproductive gonozooids. Its nematocyst venom causes extreme pain and can be life-threatening to humans. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Platygyra sinensis	PSINE2	PSINE2.png	Platygyra sinensis is a massive brain coral (family Merulinidae) forming large, dome-shaped colonies with meandering valleys and ridges across shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, from the Red Sea to the Pacific. Its labyrinthine surface with shared walls between adjacent valleys is characteristic of meandroid coral growth. The species maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae and is an important reef-builder across the Indo-Pacific. Platygyra corals are subjects of bleaching research and restoration programs across the Indo-Pacific reef zone. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Plumapathes pennacea	PPENN	PPENN.png	Plumapathes pennacea is a feather-like black coral (order Antipatharia, family Antipathidae) found in deep-water environments of the Caribbean Sea and western Atlantic, forming characteristic pinnate (feather-shaped) colonies with alternating lateral branches arising from a central axis. Like other antipatharians, it has a proteinaceous skeletal axis covered in microscopic spines and can be very long-lived, with some colonies estimated at thousands of years. Black corals are protected under CITES due to their historical overexploitation for jewelry. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Pocillopora acuta	PACUT	PACUT.png	Pocillopora acuta is a branching scleractinian coral (family Pocilloporidae) widely distributed across the Indo-Pacific, forming compact, warty colonies with characteristic verrucae (surface projections) that are diagnostic of the genus. It is an ecologically important pioneer and early-successional coral species capable of rapidly colonizing disturbed reef areas and is frequently used in coral restoration efforts. The species harbors diverse Symbiodiniaceae communities and shows high phenotypic plasticity across environmental gradients. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Pocillopora damicornis	PDAMI	PDAMI.png	Pocillopora damicornis, the cauliflower coral (family Pocilloporidae), is one of the most widespread and ecologically important branching corals in the Indo-Pacific, forming compact, cauliflower-like colonies with characteristic verrucae on exposed reef crests and slopes. It is among the most studied corals in the world, serving as a model for coral reproduction, symbiosis, and bleaching physiology. The species shows remarkable phenotypic plasticity and harbors diverse Symbiodiniaceae genotypes with variable thermal tolerance. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Pocillopora grandis	Pocillopora_grandis	Pocillopora_grandis.png	Pocillopora grandis is a robust, branching scleractinian coral (family Pocilloporidae) distributed across the tropical Indo-Pacific and eastern Pacific, forming thick-branched colonies with coarse verrucae and polyps on exposed reef crests and upper slopes. It was formerly considered part of the Pocillopora eydouxi complex and has been distinguished through molecular phylogenetics as a distinct species. The species is a dominant reef-builder in the eastern tropical Pacific and maintains Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
