Pocillopora verrucosa	PVERR	PVERR.png	Pocillopora verrucosa is a branching scleractinian coral (family Pocilloporidae) widely distributed in the Indo-Pacific and Red Sea, forming compact colonies with fine, densely packed verrucae that are diagnostic of the species. It is an ecologically dominant species in Red Sea reef communities and is among the most thermally tolerant Pocillopora species, making it important for research on coral resilience. Its population structure and bleaching dynamics are subjects of active research across the Indo-Pacific. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Podabacia crustacea	PCRUS	PCRUS.png	Podabacia crustacea is a plating to foliose scleractinian coral (family Fungiidae) found in shallow to moderately deep Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming thin encrusting or plate-like colonies attached to hard substrates—an unusual growth form within the mostly free-living or solitary Fungiidae family. The species inhabits shaded reef overhangs, slopes, and lagoons where it maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae. Its colonial growth distinguishes it from most other fungiids. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Polymyces sp. M5039	Polymyces_sp_M5039	Polymyces_sp_M5039.png	M5039 is an undescribed myxozoan parasite (class Myxosporea) assigned a provisional specimen identifier from a museum collection, representing a potentially new species within this parasitic cnidarian group. Myxozoans in general are obligate endoparasites of fish and other aquatic hosts, producing diagnostic spores with polar capsules homologous to cnidarian nematocysts. Genomic resources for undescribed myxozoan lineages are essential for understanding the full extent of parasitic cnidarian diversity and host-parasite molecular evolution.
Porites australiensis	PAUST	PAUST.png	Porites australiensis is a massive to encrusting scleractinian coral (family Poritidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming dome-shaped or plating colonies with the densely packed, small corallites characteristic of the genus Porites. The genus Porites is one of the most ecologically important and speciose coral genera in the Indo-Pacific, forming the structural backbone of many reef systems and providing records of paleoclimate in its skeletal growth bands. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Porites compressa	PCOMP	PCOMP.png	Porites compressa, the finger coral, is an endemic Hawaiian scleractinian coral (family Poritidae) forming characteristic thick, finger-like or columnar colonies in sheltered to moderately exposed reef environments. It is one of the two dominant reef-building corals in Hawaii alongside Porites lobata and is a major contributor to Hawaiian reef carbonate accretion and structural complexity. The species harbors Symbiodiniaceae and shows variable bleaching susceptibility among genotypes, making it important for Hawaiian reef resilience research. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Porites cylindrica	PCYLI	PCYLI.png	Porites cylindrica is a branching scleractinian coral (family Poritidae) found across the Indo-Pacific, forming colonies of upright cylindrical branches with the characteristically small, densely packed corallites of the genus Porites. It inhabits reef flats, lagoons, and sheltered reef environments where it can form extensive thickets providing important habitat for reef fish and invertebrates. The species is susceptible to bleaching and tissue loss disease and is studied in the context of Indo-Pacific reef degradation. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Porites divaricata	PDIVA	PDIVA.png	Porites divaricata is a branching scleractinian coral (family Poritidae) found in shallow Caribbean reef and lagoonal environments, forming colonies of thin, widely diverging branches characteristic of this species. It is a common component of Caribbean seagrass beds and lagoonal reef communities and tolerates turbid, low-flow conditions better than most other branching corals. Its tolerance of marginal reef conditions makes it an interesting model for studying coral adaptation to environmental stress and degraded habitat quality. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
