Porites lobata	PLOBA	PLOBA.png	Porites lobata, the lobe coral, is one of the most widespread and ecologically dominant massive corals in the Indo-Pacific, forming large, rounded or lobate colonies that can live for centuries and reach several meters in diameter. It serves as a natural archive of ocean temperature, salinity, and pollution history through geochemical analysis of its skeletal growth bands. The species is a dominant reef-builder in Hawaiian reefs and across the tropical Pacific. It is a primary target for long-term coral reef monitoring and paleoclimate reconstruction. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Porites lutea	PLUTE	PLUTE.png	Porites lutea is a massive scleractinian coral (family Poritidae) and one of the most dominant reef-building corals across the Indo-Pacific, from the Red Sea to the Pacific. Its large, dome-shaped colonies with characteristic lemon-yellow or pale coloration when healthy can reach several meters across and are long-lived, recording decades of environmental history in their skeletal banding. The species is one of the most thoroughly studied Porites species in terms of growth physiology, symbiont diversity, and bleaching biology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
