Ptychogena californica	Ptychogena_californica	Ptychogena_californica.png	Ptychogena californica is a hydrozoan medusa (family Ptychogeniidae) found in cold temperate and subarctic waters of the northeastern Pacific coast, a relatively delicate transparent medusa with a characteristic folded bell and marginal tentacles used for prey capture in nearshore and coastal oceanic environments. As with many hydromedusae, its benthic hydroid stage is poorly characterized, and genomic data may facilitate identification of its life cycle stages in environmental samples.
Radianthus crispa	Radianthus_crispa	Radianthus_crispa.png	Radianthus crispa is a large sea anemone (family Stichodactylidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, with a broad oral disc bearing numerous, short, crisped (curly-tipped) tentacles characteristic of this species and family. Like other stichodactylid anemones, it acts as a host to anemonefish (clownfish) of the genus Amphiprion, particularly A. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Ragactis hyalina	Ragactis_hyalina	Ragactis_hyalina.png	Ragactis hyalina is a small, transparent sea anemone (family Actiniidae) found in shallow coastal waters of the Indo-Pacific and Mediterranean Sea, typically inhabiting sandy or muddy substrates with the column partially buried and only the oral disc exposed. Its glass-like transparency in live specimens gives it its specific epithet. Its transparent body and small size may make it a candidate for laboratory model studies of cnidarian cellular biology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Renilla koellikeri	Renilla_koellikeri	Renilla_koellikeri.png	Renilla koellikeri is a sea pansy (order Pennatulacea, family Renillidae) found in shallow sandy habitats of the eastern Pacific coast, forming kidney-shaped or disc-like colonies anchored in soft sediments by a short peduncle with numerous feeding polyps covering the dorsal surface. Sea pansies are bioluminescent, producing flashes of light through a luciferase-based system using renillin as the luciferin substrate, and have been used in research on bioluminescence mechanisms. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Renilla reniformis	Renilla_reniformis	Renilla_reniformis.png	Renilla reniformis, the sea pansy (family Renillidae), is a colonial pennatulacean octocoral found in shallow sandy habitats along the Atlantic coast of North America and Caribbean, forming distinctive kidney-shaped or oval disc colonies anchored in soft sediment. It is the biological source of Renilla luciferase (RLuc), which has become one of the most widely used reporter enzymes in molecular biology and cell imaging, enabling dual-reporter assays alongside firefly luciferase. A genome has been produced for R. Its luciferase gene has been cloned and expressed in diverse biological systems for research applications.
Rhodactis osculifera	ROSCU	ROSCU.png	Rhodactis osculifera is a corallimorpharian (order Corallimorpharia, family Discosomatidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming colonial, disc-shaped polyps with distinctive tentacles organized in irregular rows across the oral disc. Corallimorpharians are phylogenetically intermediate between scleractinian corals and sea anemones, lacking a calcified skeleton but sharing many molecular and morphological characters with stony corals. The species is commonly kept in marine reef aquaria. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Rhopilema esculentum	RESCU	RESCU.png	Rhopilema esculentum, the edible jellyfish (family Rhizostomatidae), is a large rhizostome scyphozoan distributed in the coastal waters of East Asia including China, Japan, and Korea, and is one of the most commercially important jellyfish species globally, harvested extensively for human consumption as a traditional food ingredient. Its bell can reach 60 cm in diameter, and it harbors Symbiodiniaceae photosymbionts in its oral arms. Wild populations form large blooms in Chinese coastal waters with significant impacts on fisheries. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Ricordea florida	RFLOR	RFLOR.png	Ricordea florida is a corallimorpharian (order Corallimorpharia, family Ricordeidae) endemic to the Caribbean Sea, forming small, colonial disc-shaped polyps with distinctive, bead-like (capitate) tentacle tips in vivid colors ranging from green and orange to blue and purple. It is one of the most popular and valuable corallimorpharians in the marine aquarium trade due to its compact size and brilliant coloration. Unlike many reef corals, it lacks a calcareous skeleton but maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Sanderia malayensis	SMALA	SMALA.png	Sanderia malayensis is a scyphozoan jellyfish (family Pelagiidae) distributed in coastal waters of the Indo-Pacific, particularly in Southeast Asian seas, forming a moderate-sized medusa with eight marginal lappets and nematocyst-bearing tentacles capable of causing skin irritation. It is a pelagic predator feeding on zooplankton and small fish larvae in tropical coastal and estuarine environments. The species contributes to the diverse gelatinous zooplankton community of Southeast Asian coastal waters. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Sarsia tubulosa	Sarsia_tubulosa	Sarsia_tubulosa.png	Sarsia tubulosa is a hydrozoan medusa (family Corymorphidae or Tubulariidae) found in cold temperate and boreal coastal waters of the North Atlantic, with a simple, transparent bell bearing four radial canals and a distinctive long, pendant manubrium for capturing copepod prey. The species is a model for studying hydrozoan medusa development and the control of sexual reproduction by neuropeptides. Sarsia hydromedusae have been used in classical studies of cnidarian neurobiology and reproduction physiology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Scolanthus callimorphus	SCALL	SCALL.png	Scolanthus callimorphus is a tube-dwelling sea anemone (family Edwardsiidae) found in soft sediments of temperate coastal waters in the northeastern Atlantic and Mediterranean Sea, lacking a pedal disc and instead living in a mucous tube anchored in sandy or muddy substrates with only the oral disc exposed for feeding. Like other edwardsiids, it occupies a basal phylogenetic position within Actiniaria and is important for reconstructing the ancestral body plan of sea anemones. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Scolionema suvaense	Scolionema_suvaense	Scolionema_suvaense.png	Scolionema suvaense is a small coronate scyphozoan jellyfish (order Coronatae, family Linuchidae) found in shallow tropical coastal waters of the Indo-Pacific, with a distinctive bell bearing a prominent coronal groove—a diagnostic feature of coronate medusae representing the most ancestral order of Scyphozoa. The species forms small medusae that inhabit mangrove channels, lagoons, and nearshore tropical waters. Its biology highlights the ecological diversity of the ancestral scyphozoan body plan. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Sertularella gayi	Sertularella_gayi	Sertularella_gayi.png	Sertularella gayi is a colonial hydrozoan (family Sertulariidae, order Leptothecata) found in temperate coastal waters of the South Atlantic and Pacific, forming erect, branching thecate hydroid colonies on rocky substrates, shells, and macroalgae in subtidal habitats. Like other sertulariid hydroids, it produces gonotheca enclosing the gonozooids that release planula larvae or medusae for dispersal. Its wide southern hemisphere distribution makes it relevant to studies of temperate marine biogeography. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Siderastrea radians	SRADI	SRADI.png	Siderastrea radians, the lesser starlet coral (family Siderastreidae), is a small, encrusting to dome-shaped scleractinian coral endemic to the Caribbean Sea and Gulf of Mexico, typically forming small hemispherical colonies with characteristic star-shaped corallites on shallow reef pavements, rubble, and rocky substrates. The species is notably tolerant of turbid, warm, and low-salinity conditions, colonizing marginal reef environments including seagrass beds and mangrove prop roots where other corals cannot survive. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Siderastrea siderea	SSIDE	SSIDE.png	Siderastrea siderea, the massive starlet coral (family Siderastreidae), is a dominant massive reef-building coral throughout the Caribbean Sea and Gulf of Mexico, forming large, hemispherical or encrusting colonies with characteristic star-shaped corallites across a wide depth range from the intertidal zone to over 70 m. It is one of the most common Caribbean corals and notable for its resistance to bleaching and disease compared to other Caribbean species. It is an important target for coral restoration research given its relative resilience. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Sphenopus marsupialis	Sphenopus_marsupialis	Sphenopus_marsupialis.png	Sphenopus marsupialis is a solitary zoantharian (family Sphenopidae, order Zoantharia) found in shallow sandy and muddy substrates across the Indo-Pacific and Mediterranean, unusual among zoantharians in being solitary rather than colonial and living partially buried in soft sediments with only the oral disc exposed. Its single, large polyp can incorporate foreign particles from the substrate into its body wall. Its ecology and systematic position within Sphenopidae are important for understanding zoantharian body plan evolution. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Stephanocoenia intersepta	SINTE	SINTE.png	Stephanocoenia intersepta, the blushing star coral (family Astrocoeniidae), is a dome-forming scleractinian coral endemic to the Caribbean Sea, forming compact, rounded colonies with distinctive corallite morphology on shallow to mid-depth reef environments. It is the only Caribbean representative of the family Astrocoeniidae, making it systematically important for studies of Caribbean coral biogeography and the evolutionary history of reef-building corals in the Atlantic. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
