Stichodactyla helianthus	Stichodactyla_helianthus	Stichodactyla_helianthus.png	Stichodactyla helianthus, the sun anemone or Caribbean carpet anemone (family Stichodactylidae), is found in shallow reef environments of the Caribbean Sea and tropical western Atlantic, forming compact, sun-flower-like oral discs with numerous short tentacles attached to rocky surfaces and rubble. Unlike its Indo-Pacific congeners, it does not typically host clownfish due to the absence of Amphiprion from the Atlantic, but it is associated with several endemic commensal shrimp and fish species. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Stichodactyla mertensii	Stichodactyla_mertensii	Stichodactyla_mertensii.png	Stichodactyla mertensii, Mertens' carpet anemone (family Stichodactylidae), is the largest carpet anemone in the world, with oral discs potentially reaching 1 m or more in exposed Indo-Pacific reef environments, bearing thousands of short, thick tentacles with distinctive coloration. It hosts the largest diversity of clownfish species of any anemone host, with at least twelve Amphiprion species known to associate with it. Its ecology and physiology have been studied in the context of reef anemone population dynamics and climate vulnerability. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
