Stylophora pistillata	SPIST	SPIST.png	Stylophora pistillata is a branching scleractinian coral (family Pocilloporidae) widely distributed in the Indo-Pacific and Red Sea, forming compact, rounded colonies with characteristic blunt-tipped branches and is one of the most thoroughly studied corals for calcification physiology, biomineralization mechanisms, and bleaching biology. The species exhibits remarkable phenotypic plasticity across depth and light gradients and is extensively used in laboratory experiments due to its tolerance of aquarium conditions. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Tamoya ohboya	Tamoya_ohboya	Tamoya_ohboya.png	Tamoya ohboya is a cubozoan box jellyfish (family Carybdeidae) described from Caribbean waters, named informally for the exclamation of pain its sting produces in swimmers. Like other carybdeid box jellyfish, it possesses four paddle-shaped pedalia each bearing a single tentacle with powerful nematocysts capable of causing significant dermatological reactions. Its complex visual system includes four sets of eyes at each corner of the bell, with both simple pit eyes and complex lens eyes providing sophisticated spatial vision. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Telmatactis stephensoni	TSTEP	TSTEP.png	Telmatactis stephensoni is a sea anemone (family Isophellidae) found in shallow tropical reef environments of the Indo-Pacific and Caribbean, characterized by a robust column with distinctive mesenterial arrangements and nematocyst-laden tentacles for prey capture inhabiting crevices and rubble zones. Its placement within Actiniaria has been clarified through molecular phylogenetic analyses, revealing relationships to other shallow tropical anemone families. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Thalassianthus aster	Thalassianthus_aster	Thalassianthus_aster.png	Thalassianthus aster is a sea anemone (family Thalassianthidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming a distinctive star-shaped oral disc with radiating rows of tentacles organized in a pattern unique among actiniarians. The species inhabits sandy and rubble substrates at the base of reefs where it partially buries its column. Like other thalassianthids, it may host commensal shrimps and fish. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Thelohanellus kitauei	TKITA	TKITA.png	Thelohanellus kitauei is a freshwater myxozoan parasite (family Myxobolidae) infecting the intestinal wall of common carp (Cyprinus carpio) in East Asia, causing intestinal thelohanellosis characterized by large, visible cysts in the gut wall that cause significant morbidity and mortality in infected fish, particularly in Chinese and Japanese carp aquaculture. Its genome revealed extensive gene loss and metabolic pathway reduction consistent with obligate intracellular parasitism.
Tiaropsis multicirrata	Tiaropsis_multicirrata	Tiaropsis_multicirrata.png	Tiaropsis multicirrata is a leptomedusa hydrozoan (family Eirenidae, order Leptothecata) found in cold temperate to boreal coastal waters of the North Pacific and North Atlantic, a transparent, disk-shaped hydromedusa with numerous evenly spaced tentacles around the bell margin and clusters of statocysts for orientation in the water column. The species is a planktonic predator of small copepods and other zooplankton, and its transparent body and relative abundance make it a tractable subject for laboratory studies of hydrozoan medusa physiology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Trachythela sp. YZ-2020	TSP	TSP.png	Cold-water corals Trachythela sp. YZ-2020 are important habitats for creatures in the deep-sea environment, but they have been degraded by anthropogenic activity. They live in the cold, dark, and hypoxic deep-sea waters and are widespread around the world. Most of them must attach to hard-bottom substrates to grow, and only a few can live on soft sediments. Due to the lack of symbiotic zooxanthellae, they do not require sunlight as a source of energy, and their major source of nutrients is the microscopic zooplankton that comes from passing currents or descends from the surface of the ocean.
Tripedalia maipoensis	TMAIP	TMAIP.png	Tripedalia maipoensis is a small cubozoan box jellyfish (family Tripedaliidae) found in shallow mangrove and lagoon habitats of the Indo-Pacific and Caribbean, characterized by a small, cube-shaped bell bearing three pedalia per corner, each with a single tentacle, and complex multi-lens eyes capable of spatial vision. The species was the subject of pioneering research on cubozoan visual ecology demonstrating that box jellyfish can use their sophisticated eyes to navigate through mangrove prop roots. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Tubastraea coccinea	TCOCC	TCOCC.png	Tubastraea coccinea, the orange cup coral (family Dendrophylliidae), is an azooxanthellate colonial scleractinian coral native to the Indo-Pacific but invasive in the Caribbean and Gulf of Mexico, where it has spread rapidly since its introduction in the 1940s and now threatens native benthic communities by outcompeting sponges, gorgonians, and native corals for space. Its large, bright orange polyps lack photosymbionts and feed exclusively on zooplankton and suspended organic matter. It is considered one of the most invasive marine organisms in the Atlantic basin. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Turbinaria reniformis	TRENI	TRENI.png	Turbinaria reniformis is a foliose to funnel-shaped scleractinian coral (family Dendrophylliidae) found in shallow to moderately deep Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming distinctive scroll-like, funnel-shaped, or plate-like colonies with large polyps, and is unique in the family for being zooxanthellate. The species inhabits turbid, low-light reef environments where its unusual morphology maximizes light collection by Symbiodiniaceae. It is studied in the context of coral adaptation to turbid coastal reef conditions. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Turritopsis dohrnii	TDOHR	TDOHR.png	Turritopsis dohrnii, the immortal jellyfish (family Oceaniidae), is a small hydrozoan medusa renowned for its unique ability to revert from the sexually mature medusa stage back to the juvenile polyp stage after stress or senescence—a process of transdifferentiation enabling indefinite repetition of its life cycle and making it the only known animal capable of biological immortality at the individual level. Distributed widely in temperate and tropical oceans globally through shipping ballast water, it has become a cosmopolitan species. It is an important model for aging and regeneration biology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Turritopsis rubra	TRUBR	TRUBR.png	Turritopsis rubra is a small, coastal hydromedusa (family Oceaniidae) native to the shallow, wave-exposed neritic waters of New Zealand and southern Australia. Morphologically, the adult medusa is tiny and bell-shaped, measuring up to 7 mm in height, and features a transparent exumbrella that vividly displays an intensively crimson-red cruciform stomach and gonads within the bell cavity. The subumbrella margin anchors a dense, crowded arrangement of up to 120 simple tentacles originating from distinct, closely spaced tentacle bulbs equipped with light-sensitive adaxial ocelli. Unlike its globally renowned congener Turritopsis dohrnii (the "immortal jellyfish"), mature medusae of T. rubra lack the robust biological immortality phenotype to successfully undergo life cycle reversion back into a juvenile polyp under physiological stress. Genomically, its fully decoded chromosome-level reference framework serves as a critical evolutionary baseline; comparative genomic mapping has revealed that T. rubra possesses significantly fewer duplication variants of DNA repair polymerases, telomere maintenance pathways, and oxidative stress mitigation coding matrices compared to T. dohrnii. Furthermore, T. rubra displays a specialized reproductive ecology, utilizing gonochoristic fertilization where females exhibit a brooding habit, retaining fertilized eggs internally until they develop into ciliated planula larvae before ocean dispersal.
Umimayanthus chanpuru	Umimayanthus_chanpuru	Umimayanthus_chanpuru.png	Umimayanthus chanpuru is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) found in tropical Indo-Pacific waters, growing epizoically on hydroid colonies with small polyps characteristic of this unusual cnidarian-on-cnidarian lifestyle. The species name 'chanpuru' refers to a traditional Okinawan stir-fry dish, reflecting its discovery in Okinawan waters. Its genome provides material for investigating host specificity, secondary metabolite production, and the molecular evolution of epizoic lifestyles in this phylogenetically distinctive zoantharian family.
Umimayanthus kanabou	Umimayanthus_kanabou	Umimayanthus_kanabou.png	Umimayanthus kanabou is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) found in tropical Pacific waters, forming epizoic colonies on hydroid branches in a lifestyle unique among zoantharians. Like other Umimayanthus species, it has small, gracile polyps adapted to colonizing delicate hydroid hosts. The molecular systematics of Umimayanthus and related genera within Hydrozoanthidae are being progressively resolved through whole-genome phylogenetic approaches.
