Xenia sp. Carnegie-2017	XSP	XSP.png	Xenia sp. Carnegie-2017 is an undescribed soft coral (order Alcyonacea, family Xeniidae) assigned a provisional specimen identifier from a Carnegie Institution collection, representing a potentially new species within the genus Xenia. Xeniid soft corals are distinctive for their characteristic pulsing behavior—rhythmic contractions of the polyps that are thought to enhance photosynthesis by circulating water and improving gas exchange around the Symbiodiniaceae-laden tissues. The genus Xenia is popular in reef aquaria due to its dynamic movement. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Zoanthus gigantus	Zoanthus_gigantus	Zoanthus_gigantus.png	Zoanthus gigantus is a large-polyped zoantharian (family Zoanthidae, order Zoantharia) found in shallow tropical Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming encrusting colonial mats with notably large polyps compared to most other Zoanthus species—a characteristic reflected in its specific epithet. Like other Zoanthus species, it lacks a calcareous skeleton and forms flat encrusting colonies on hard substrates, reef rubble, and dead coral. Zoanthus species are popular in the marine aquarium trade and are studied for their diverse secondary metabolite chemistry. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Zoanthus pulchellus	Zoanthus_pulchellus	Zoanthus_pulchellus.png	Zoanthus pulchellus is a highly colonial, photosynthetic zoanthid (family Zoanthidae) widely distributed across intertidal and shallow subtidal rocky shores, rubble zones, and reef flats within the tropical Western Atlantic, Caribbean Sea, and parts of the Indo-Pacific. Morphologically, the species is characterized by forming exceptionally dense, compact mat-like clonal colonies (sea mats) where individual columnar polyps press tightly against one another. The polyps are relatively short to moderately elongated, typically ranging from 4 to 30 mm (occasionally reaching 50 mm) in height and 4 to 15 mm in diameter, with a circular to polygonal cross-section when fully expanded. The oral disc exhibits broad chromatic variations, frequently displaying shades of yellowish-green, fluorescent green, bluish-purple, or brown, and is fringed by a double row of 60 to 70 short, blunt marginal tentacles. Unlike the related genus Palythoa, the tissue architecture of Z. pulchellus completely lacks sand or sediment encrustation within its coenenchyme and mesoglea. Ecologically, it maintains an obligate photo-symbiosis with endosymbiotic zooxanthellae for primary carbon fixation. Genomically, Z. pulchellus has been successfully sequenced via short-read platforms (suborder Brachycnemina initiative), providing an important baseline assembly to investigate transposable element (TE) landscapes, the evolutionary dynamics of repeatome expansion, and the biosynthesis of complex marine alkaloids such as norzoanthamine.
Zoanthus sansibaricus	Zoanthus_sansibaricus	Zoanthus_sansibaricus.png	Zoanthus sansibaricus is a colonial zoantharian (family Zoanthidae) distributed in shallow reef environments of the western Indian Ocean and East African coast, including around Zanzibar (Sansibar) from which it takes its name, forming encrusting mats on reef rock and dead coral substrates. The species is part of the diverse Indo-Pacific zoanthid fauna and maintains photosymbiotic relationships with Symbiodiniaceae in the shallow, well-lit reef environments it inhabits. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Zoanthus sociatus	Zoanthus_sociatus	Zoanthus_sociatus.png	Zoanthus sociatus is a colonial zoantharian (family Zoanthidae) widely distributed in shallow Caribbean reef and rocky environments, forming dense, sociable mats of polyps on exposed reef crests, rubble, and intertidal zones across the tropical western Atlantic. It is one of the most abundant and ecologically dominant zoantharians in the Caribbean, competing effectively with algae and other encrusting organisms for space on degraded reef surfaces. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Acropora abrotanoides	Acropora_abrotanoides	Acropora_abrotanoides.png	Acropora abrotanoides is a corymbose to tabular scleractinian coral (family Acroporidae) distributed in Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming spreading plate-like colonies with compact branches and maintaining Symbiodiniaceae photosymbiosis. Its genomic resources contribute to the expanding comparative genomic database for the genus Acropora, supporting studies of coral evolution, hybridization, and bleaching susceptibility across this ecologically dominant coral genus. It is listed as Vulnerable by the IUCN.
Alvinactis sp. YZ-2022	Alvinactis_sp_YZ_2022	Alvinactis_sp_YZ_2022.png	Alvinactis sp. YZ-2022 is an undescribed deep-sea sea anemone (family Actiniidae) collected from hydrothermal vent or cold seep environments in the western Pacific, assigned a provisional collection identifier from a 2022 deep-sea survey. Like other Alvinactis species, it is adapted to the chemosynthetic ecosystem conditions of vent habitats and relies on capturing vent-associated fauna rather than photosymbiosis for nutrition. Its genomic characterization provides comparative data for understanding molecular adaptations to hydrothermal vent environments.
Chrysaora pentastoma	Chrysaora_pentastoma	Chrysaora_pentastoma.png	Chrysaora pentastoma is a scyphozoan jellyfish (family Pelagiidae) found in the tropical and subtropical Indo-Pacific, with five arms rather than the four characteristic of most Chrysaora species—a distinctive morphological feature reflected in its specific epithet (penta = five, stoma = mouth). It inhabits tropical coastal and open-ocean waters and is a planktonic predator of zooplankton and small fish. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Muricea laxa	Muricea_laxa	Muricea_laxa.png	Muricea laxa is a loosely branching gorgonian coral (family Plexauridae) found on shallow Caribbean reef environments, its specific epithet ('laxa' meaning loose or open) reflecting its less densely branched colony architecture compared to other Muricea species. Its colonies inhabit reef crests and upper slopes contributing to the structural diversity of Caribbean gorgonian communities. Like other Caribbean plexaurids, it produces terpenoid compounds with documented bioactive properties. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Platygyra daedalea	Platygyra_daedalea	Platygyra_daedalea.png	Platygyra daedalea, the maze or daedal brain coral (family Merulinidae), is a massive, brain-like scleractinian coral distributed widely across the Indo-Pacific from the Red Sea to the central Pacific, forming large dome-shaped or encrusting colonies with characteristic meandering ridge-and-valley patterns named for the mythological labyrinth-builder Daedalus. The species is an important reef-builder across the Indo-Pacific and maintains photosymbiosis with Symbiodiniaceae. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Rhizostoma luteum	Rhizostoma_luteum	Rhizostoma_luteum.png	Rhizostoma luteum is a large rhizostome scyphozoan jellyfish (family Rhizostomatidae) found in the Mediterranean Sea and northeastern Atlantic, forming a compact, dome-shaped bell with a distinctive yellow to pale coloration. Like other rhizostomes, it lacks marginal tentacles and feeds through mouth openings on its highly branched oral arms. The species forms seasonal blooms in Mediterranean coastal waters and is an important component of gelatinous zooplankton communities. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Sarcophyton elegans	Sarcophyton_elegans	Sarcophyton_elegans.png	Sarcophyton elegans is a mushroom leather coral (order Alcyonacea, family Alcyoniidae) found in shallow Indo-Pacific reef environments, forming characteristic mushroom-shaped or toadstool-like colonies with a stalk and broad, folded cap covered in feeding polyps. The genus Sarcophyton produces a diverse array of cembranoid diterpenes, secondary metabolites with documented cytotoxic, anti-inflammatory, and antifungal properties of significant pharmaceutical interest. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Actinoscyphia sp. M1220	Actinoscyphia_sp_m1220	Actinoscyphia_sp_m1220.png	Actinoscyphia sp. M1220 is an unclassified deep-sea box-headed or Venus flytrap sea anemone (family Actinoscyphiidae) primarily found in bathyal and abyssal benthic habitats of the world's oceans. Characterized by a thick, carnified column and a unique bilobed oral disc fringed with numerous short, capitate tentacles, this suspension-feeding cnidarian exhibits a distinct predatory morphology mimicking a Venus flytrap. The species utilizes an active closure mechanism: when macro-organic debris or small motile invertebrates trigger the specialized nematocysts on its tentacles, the oral disc rapidly folds inward along a central axis to trap and digest the prey. Genomic and molecular barcoding data for this specific operational taxonomic unit (OTU) are actively maintained in public databases like NCBI, serving as crucial reference points for exploring deep-sea cnidarian biodiversity, evolutionary adaptation to extreme pressure, and benthic community ecology.
Ectopleura larynx	Ectopleura_larynx	Ectopleura_larynx.png	Ectopleura larynx is a colonial, athecate hydroid (family Tubulariidae) widely distributed in shallow, wave-exposed rocky shores and artificial structures across the North Atlantic and North Sea. Morphologically, it forms dense, pinkish tufts characterized by unbranched or sparingly branched corneous hydrocauli that support large, solitary hydranths. Each flower-like hydranth features two distinct, concentric whorls of pale pink to reddish tentacles: an outer ring of long aboral tentacles and an inner cluster of shorter oral tentacles surrounding the mouth. Characterized by a direct life cycle, E. larynx lacks a free-swimming medusa stage; instead, it develops fixed gonophores (sporosacs) that release ciliated actinula larvae directly into the water column. As a notorious marine biofouling organism, this species exhibits exceptional capabilities for rapid recruitment and space competition, frequently infesting coastal aquaculture infrastructure, ship hulls, and marine energy installations.
Coelastrea aspera	Coelastrea_aspera	Coelastrea_aspera.png	Coelastrea aspera is a colonial, hermatypic scleractinian coral (family Merulinidae) widely distributed across shallow, wave-sheltered or high-sediment reef flats, lagoons, and intertidal rocky shores throughout the Indo-Pacific region. Morphologically, it typically forms massive, encrusting, or subhemispherical colonies characterized by a distinctive cerioid corallite arrangement, where adjacent corallites share fused, common walls. The corallites are relatively small, polygonal (often angular or subquadrate), and feature deep calices with high, sharply defined septa that impart a notably rough, textured appearance to the skeletal surface. Living colonies exhibit a range of dull colorations, predominantly shades of pale brown, grey-green, or brownish-yellow, frequently displaying contrasting pale or vibrant green oral discs. Characterized by an environmental tolerance for extreme conditions, C. aspera is a broadcast spawning species that synchronizedly releases gametes into the water column for external fertilization and subsequent planktonic planula larval development. As a resilient and ecologically vital reef-building component, this species possesses an exceptional capability to withstand high solar radiation, temperature fluctuations, and heavy sedimentation, making it a dominant and highly competitive pioneer organism in challenging intertidal zones and degraded coral reef habitats
Acropora cf. manni	Acropora_cf_manni	-	Its colonies display corymbose to tabular forms occupying diverse reef zones across the Pacific. Like all acroporids, it relies on endosymbiotic Symbiodiniaceae for photosynthetic carbon and is susceptible to thermal bleaching. Taxonomic uncertainty makes this lineage valuable for integrative morphological and genomic species delimitation.
Alatinidae sp. Z8VKAUB7J3	Alatinidae_sp_Z8VKAUB7J3	-	Alatinidae sp. Z8VKAUB7J3 is an undescribed cubozoan jellyfish (family Alatinidae) assigned a provisional barcode identifier from a Smithsonian Natural History Museum specimen, representing a potentially distinct lineage within this family of box jellyfish. Cubozoans are distinguished by their box-shaped bell, corner-mounted tentacle clusters, and highly complex lens eyes, inhabiting tropical to subtropical marine environments. Their potent nematocyst-based venoms are of significant medical importance. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Ceratomyxa sp. 58-BR2022	Ceratomyxa_sp_58_BR2022	-	Ceratomyxa sp. 58-BR2022 is an undescribed myxozoan parasite (family Ceratomyxidae) recovered from marine fish in Brazil, assigned a provisional barcode identifier, and closely related to or potentially conspecific with Ceratomyxa sp. Like other Ceratomyxa, it is an obligate intracellular parasite of marine fish infecting bile ducts or gallbladder epithelial cells and producing diagnostic ellipsoidal spores with polar capsules homologous to cnidarian nematocysts. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Ceratonova shasta	Ceratonova_shasta	-	Ceratonova shasta (formerly Ceratomyxa shasta) is a freshwater myxozoan parasite (family Ceratomyxidae) notorious as a significant pathogen of salmonid fish in the Pacific Northwest of North America, infecting the intestinal epithelium and causing severe ceratonova disease with high mortality in juvenile fish. The parasite requires a polychaete worm (Manayunkia occidentalis) as an alternate host to complete its life cycle in rivers such as the Klamath and Trinity Rivers. It is a fish pathogen of major concern to salmon conservation and hatchery management. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Ellipsomyxa sp. 115-BR2022	Ellipsomyxa_sp_115_BR2022	-	Ellipsomyxa sp. 115-BR2022 is a myxozoan parasite (family Ceratomyxidae) assigned a provisional designation from a Brazilian survey of marine fish parasites, typically infecting the gallbladder of marine teleost fish and producing diagnostic spores with polar capsules that inject polar filaments into host tissues. Like other myxozoans, these organisms have highly reduced body plans resulting from extreme parasitic reduction but retain cnidarian-type features at the genomic level.
Ellipsomyxa sp. 70-BR2022	Ellipsomyxa_sp_70_BR2022	-	Ellipsomyxa sp. 70-BR2022 is an undescribed myxozoan parasite (family Ceratomyxidae) recovered from marine fish in Brazil, assigned a provisional barcode identifier. Myxozoans are obligate endoparasites with drastically reduced genomes and body plans, historically classified as protozoa but now recognized as highly derived cnidarians based on molecular phylogenetics. Species of Ellipsomyxa infect the bile of marine teleost fish and produce characteristic ellipsoidal spores transmitted to alternate polychaete worm hosts.
Enteromyxum leei	Enteromyxum_leei	-	Enteromyxum leei is a myxozoan parasite (family Enteromyxidae) causing enteromyxosis in farmed marine fish, particularly gilthead sea bream (Sparus aurata) and other sparid fishes in Mediterranean aquaculture. The parasite infects the intestinal epithelium, causing severe anorexia, weight loss, and high mortality in affected fish, representing a major economic problem for European aquaculture. It was among the first myxozoans to have its genome sequenced, allowing characterization of its highly reduced gene content and the loss of many metabolic pathways typical of free-living animals, while confirming phylogenetic affiliation with Cnidaria.
Kudoa iwatai	Kudoa_iwatai	-	Kudoa iwatai is a myxozoan parasite (family Kudoidae) infecting the muscle tissue of a wide range of marine fish species, causing kudosis characterized by post-mortem liquefaction of the flesh, a serious problem for fish quality in aquaculture and commercial fisheries. The parasite produces distinctive star-shaped spores in heavily infected muscle, which release proteolytic enzymes following the death of the host fish. Its genome was sequenced and published (2017), providing one of the early complete myxozoan genomes and enabling comparative analysis of myxozoan metabolic pathway loss. Kudoa species are among the most economically important myxozoan parasites of commercial marine fish globally.
Kudoa neothunni	Kudoa_neothunni	-	Kudoa neothunni is a myxozoan parasite (family Kudoidae) infecting the muscle of yellowfin tuna (Thunnus albacares) and related scombrids, causing post-mortem muscle liquefaction and rendering infected fish unmarketable in sashimi and fresh fish markets. Its characteristic spores are small and quadrangular, deposited in muscle pseudocysts that break down after the fish dies. Economic losses from Kudoa-mediated post-harvest muscle deterioration are significant in tuna fisheries worldwide. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Kudoa sp. trachurus	Kudoa_sp_trachurus	-	Like other Kudoa species, Kudoa sp. trachurus produces intramuscular spore cysts that release proteolytic enzymes following the death of the host fish, causing post-mortem myoliquefaction that is commercially unacceptable. Genomic resources for this and related Kudoa species are important for understanding the molecular biology of post-harvest fish quality deterioration and developing detection tools for fisheries management.
Myxobolus honghuensis	MHONG	-	Myxobolus honghuensis is a freshwater myxozoan parasite (family Myxobolidae) infecting cyprinid fish in China, causing significant disease in carp aquaculture by forming myxospores with characteristic polar capsules in muscle or connective tissue, leading to lesions and reduced fish quality. Like other Myxobolus species, it requires an alternate oligochaete worm host to complete its life cycle in freshwater systems. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Myxobolus sp. r MT-2024	Myxobolus_sp_r_MT_2024	-	Myxobolus sp. r MT-2024 is an unclassified operational taxonomic unit (OTU) within the parasitic genus Myxobolus (family Myxobolidae), a highly derived group of microscopic cnidarians that have transitioned to an obligate endoparasitic lifestyle. Chronologically sequenced and analyzed in early 2024, this specific strain represents a critical breakthrough in myxozoan comparative genomics. Morphologically, it bypasses the multicellular polyp architecture of classical cnidarians, existing instead as macroscopic coelozoic or histozoic capsules (plasmodia) inside its freshwater fish host, where it produces specialized myxospores enclosed by hardened valves and equipped with distinctive polar capsules containing coiled polar filaments. Genomically, Myxobolus sp. r MT-2024 gained prominent scientific attention for possessing an exceptionally massive genome size for a myxozoan (estimated around 185 Mb), which is characterized by an ultra-high repetition rate exceeding 68% due to transposable element expansions like the MULE-MuDR. This reference asset provides fundamental clues regarding gene family expansions (such as serine proteases and hexokinases) alongside a heavily reduced mitochondrial genome, serving as a primary genomic model to dissect host-parasite interactions and the extreme evolutionary simplification within metazoans.
Myxobolus squamalis	MSQUA	-	Myxobolus squamalis is a myxozoan parasite (family Myxobolidae) infecting the scales and skin of salmonid fish in the Pacific Northwest of North America, causing squamatosis characterized by scale pitting and erosion in affected fish. The parasite produces distinctive spores embedded in scale tissue and transmitted between fish hosts and alternate oligochaete worm hosts. Understanding the biology of scale-infecting myxozoans is relevant to aquaculture health management. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Myxosporea sp. 108-BR2022	Myxosporea_sp_108_BR2022	-	Myxosporea sp. 108-BR2022 is an unclassified operational taxonomic unit (OTU) within the class Myxosporea (subphylum Myxozoa), a highly derived radiation of microscopically simplified endoparasitic cnidarians. Formally sequenced and cataloged in public registries under GenBank GCA_040939035.1, this species represents a milestone in metazoan evolutionary biology. Due to the absence of direct photographic or diagnostic spore records during rapid fieldwork sampling, the specimen is provisionally assigned as an unclassified myxosporean. Morphologically, it bypasses the macroscopic tissue plans of free-living watermouths or anemones, exhibiting an obligate parasitic lifestyle within fish tissue hosts where it develops multi-valved myxospores containing cellular polar capsules. Genomically, Myxosporea sp. 108-BR2022 possesses a high-quality, PCR-free 162 Mb Oxford Nanopore long-read assembly (Project PRJNA1107121). This reference asset gained global prominence by proving the functional presence of active DNA methylation within GC-rich regions of myxozoan gene bodies, definitively refuting the long-held hypothesis of universal epigenetic tool loss during evolutionary simplification.
Paracalyptrophora sp. SY255-jin	Paracalyptrophora_sp_SY255_jin	-	Paracalyptrophora sp. SY255-jin is an undescribed deep-water gorgonian coral (family Primnoidae, order Alcyonacea) collected from bathyal environments in Chinese waters and assigned a provisional collection identifier. Primnoidae is a diverse family of cold-water gorgonian corals found at depths of hundreds to thousands of meters, forming important structural habitats in deep-sea ecosystems. Genomic data for deep-sea gorgonians are important for understanding the evolution of cold-water coral communities increasingly threatened by deep-sea resource extraction.
Sphaeromyxa zaharoni	Sphaeromyxa_zaharoni	-	Sphaeromyxa zaharoni is a myxozoan parasite (family Sphaeromyxidae) infecting the gallbladder of marine fish in the Mediterranean Sea and adjacent Atlantic, producing characteristic elongated, fusiform spores with two polar capsules. Myxozoans are obligate endoparasites phylogenetically allied with Cnidaria, and Sphaeromyxa represents an early-diverging lineage important for reconstructing myxozoan phylogeny. Its Mediterranean distribution and fish host specificity make it relevant to marine aquaculture health research in that region. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Sphaerospora molnari	Sphaerospora_molnari	-	Sphaerospora molnari is a freshwater myxozoan parasite (family Sphaerosporidae) infecting cyprinid fish, particularly common carp (Cyprinus carpio), in European freshwater systems and causing sphaerosporosis characterized by kidney and gill pathology in juvenile fish. Like other sphaerosporeans, it alternates between a fish host where it develops myxospores and an alternate oligochaete worm host producing actinospores. It is an economically significant pathogen in European carp aquaculture. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Taxipathes sp. SY275-mao	Taxipathes_sp_SY275_mao	-	Taxipathes sp. SY275-mao is an undescribed black coral (order Antipatharia) collected from deep-water environments in Chinese waters and assigned a provisional collection identifier. Like other antipatharians, it forms colonies with a proteinaceous, spine-bearing dark axis covered by a thin layer of coenenchyme bearing small polyps, inhabiting depths of hundreds to thousands of meters. Genomic data from undescribed antipatharian specimens help clarify the systematics and biogeography of this taxonomically complex order.
Umimayanthus nakama	Umimayanthus_nakama	-	Umimayanthus nakama is a zoantharian (family Hydrozoanthidae) described from Okinawan waters, its species name 'nakama' meaning 'friend' or 'companion' in Japanese and Okinawan dialect, reflecting its commensal association with hydroid hosts. Like other members of the genus, it grows epizoically on hydroid colonies in tropical Indo-Pacific marine environments. Its inclusion in multi-species genomic datasets advances understanding of the cnidarian-cnidarian epizoic associations unique to this zoantharian family.
Umimayanthus sp. GHC0000340	Umimayanthus_sp_GHC0000340	-	Umimayanthus sp. GHC0000340 is an unclassified operational taxonomic unit (OTU) within the specialized genus Umimayanthus (family Parazoanthidae), a lineage of macrocnemic zoantharians predominantly renowned for their obligate mutualistic symbioses with marine sponges. Chronologically cataloged under the specimen code GHC0000340, this marine organism avoids the substrate attachment of typical hexacorals, opting instead to encrust and embed its colony within the living organic matrix of its host sponge. Morphologically, it forms creeping stolons or thin, mat-like coenenchyme supporting distinct, elongated tubular polyps decorated with smooth marginal tentacles. To compensate for a reduced calcium carbonate skeleton, its columnar tissue actively incorporates ambient sediment and spicules for mechanical fortification.
Zoanthus sanctaehelenae	Zoanthus_sanctaehelenae	-	Zoanthus sanctaehelenae is a zoantharian (family Zoanthidae) endemic or closely associated with the remote South Atlantic island of Saint Helena, forming encrusting colonial mats on rocky substrates in the shallow subtidal zone. As an island-endemic or near-endemic species, it provides an interesting subject for studies of marine biogeography and isolation-driven diversification in the South Atlantic. Its isolation in the South Atlantic makes it relevant to studies of island endemism in cnidarians. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
Zoanthus solanderi	Zoanthus_solanderi	-	Zoanthus solanderi is a colonial zoantharian (family Zoanthidae) found in shallow Caribbean and tropical western Atlantic reef environments, forming encrusting mats of polyps on hard substrates from intertidal rocks to shallow reef slopes. Named after the botanist Daniel Solander, it is one of the classic Caribbean zoanthid species studied since the early days of coral reef biology. Genomic data have been generated for this species and are available in public databases, enabling comparative studies of cnidarian evolution and ecology.
